\par\par \b \uc2 Verizon\uc2 's Leader Dials Up Talk of AI, Job Losses\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter and Patience Haggin \par \uc2 1,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For a big-company CEO with big AI ambitions, \uc2 Verizon Communications\uc2 ' \uc2 Dan Schulman\uc2  doesn't pull punches about the pain the technology could unleash on America's workforce.\par \par \uc2 Just months into the job, he has predicted 20% to 30% unemployment within the next two to five years. He warns that advancements in humanoid robots could upend the manual-labor jobs still seen as safe today.\par \par \uc2 And he has pushed for more education and reskilling to help workers adapt to the intensifying tech disruption.\par \par \uc2 Couched in the blunt AI talk is a warning for other CEOs: Be candid about the coming disruption, or risk a public backlash.\par \par \uc2 "It's a very difficult time, and everyone knows it is," Schulman said in an interview. "So I think being authentic, being realistic, telling the truth, as best you can" is key. That belief, he said, is why \uc2 Verizon\uc2  created a $20 million career-transition and retraining fund for the "age of AI" when the company began laying off 13,000 workers last year.\par \par \uc2 "Change is necessary, but it can be difficult," he wrote to staff then.\par \par \uc2 The warnings are a departure from the messaging of other public-company CEOs, many of whom have been bullish about AI's potential to unlock new levels of growth but demur on, or even reject, the idea of job losses.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of people are saying AI is coming, we're going to run out of jobs -- it's exactly the opposite," \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jensen Huang\uc2  said last month, pointing out that every other technological advancement has brought more productivity and prosperity. \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  CEO \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2  is similarly sanguine about potential job losses to AI. Though some roles will be replaced, "there will be other jobs created," he told CNBC in February.\par \par \uc2 In the short term, though, a cavalcade of companies from Snap to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  have invoked AI or a desire to find efficiencies as they slash large portions of their workforces. Block, which cut nearly half of its staff, predicted other companies would soon follow suit. A new \uc2 Boston Consulting Group\uc2  report predicts that AI will reshape roughly half of U.S. jobs in two to three years and that up to 15% of jobs could eventually be eliminated outright.\par \par \uc2 Many Americans fear that will happen, too. In a \uc2 Quinnipiac University\uc2  survey of 1,400 U.S. adults in March, 55% said they felt AI would bring more harm than good, up from 44% in a poll last year.\par \par \uc2 That anti-AI sentiment has fueled protests over the building of energy-guzzling data centers to run the technology -- and even violence: The suspect in a Molotov cocktail-style attack at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  CEO \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2 's California home called for "Luigi-ing some tech CEOs" months before, a reference to the 2024 fatal street shooting of a \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2  executive.\par \par \uc2 "CEOs are not thinking about this in the right way," said \uc2 Bill George\uc2 , the former CEO of \uc2 Medtronic\uc2 , who is now an executive fellow at \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Too many, he said, are focusing on productivity instead of laying out a strategy for how companies can find new business models to grow or on how workers can best use AI. "They should be very candid with them and paint the big picture."\par \par \uc2 Schulman's big picture has also included sweeping job cuts. The 13,000 layoffs he announced shortly after his appointment as CEO in October were \uc2 Verizon\uc2 's largest ever -- but necessary to make \uc2 Verizon\uc2  more efficient, he said.\par \par \uc2 Altogether, he is seeking to cut $9 billion in costs. \uc2 Verizon\uc2  has said its layoffs weren't related to AI.\par \par \uc2 The carrier was "too hierarchical, way too bureaucratic, way too process-oriented as opposed to outcomes-oriented," Schulman told investors at a \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  event last month.\par \par \uc2 In meetings, he has repeatedly told \uc2 Verizon\uc2  staff they must embrace AI, describing it as core to the company's future. He used it himself to comb through some 8,000 responses after asking employees how he could reduce bureaucracy, he said.\par \par \uc2 Schulman's embrace of AI goes deeper than cost-cutting. He envisions a company wholly reshaped by the technology, from improved customer service to more personalized options for consumers.\par \par \uc2 And he has encouraged staffers to talk to their children about AI at the dinner table. In one all-hands, Schulman recommended that staff ask AI to write their obituary to see how the technology works and how it frames their lives. He has also invited staffers to experiment with AI by writing poems to their loved ones. (He said he has done the same for his wife.)\par \par \uc2 Some employees responded by using AI to write poems for Schulman -- and they weren't bad, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Like it or not, we live in the age of AI. I happen to like it," he said in the interview.\par \par \uc2 "It's like we all wanted to live in the Renaissance or, like, when fire was first invented -- how cool would that be?" he continued. "We're in that stage. We're just not appreciating it for what it could be."\par \par \uc2 Some prominent CEOs are starting to join Schulman in acknowledging AI's potential for disruption.\par \par \uc2 After the attack on his California home, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's Altman wrote that public anxiety about AI was justified since it would usher in more change than society has perhaps ever experienced. Just days before, OpenAI also floated the idea of a four-day workweek and other policies "to keep people first."\par \par \uc2 News Corp, owner of the Journal, has a content-licensing partnership with OpenAI.\par \par \uc2 Others have also recently sounded warnings. "There's a real risk artificial intelligence could widen wealth inequality," BlackRock CEO Larry Fink wrote in his annual letter to shareholders last month. JPMorgan Chase Chief Executive Jamie Dimon recently told investors that AI's productivity gains could lead to other derivative effects.\par \par \uc2 "It may happen faster than we can adjust to it," he cautioned.\par \par \uc2 Schulman said AI may reach human-level ability, known in the industry as AGI, or artificial general intelligence, by the end of next year -- on the early side of most industry predictions. Quantum computing will come next, he said, and "change everything again." The rise of humanlike robots may not be far behind, either.\par \par \uc2 "That's definitely the world that we're in right now," Schulman said. "It's really fast, fast changing."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260420em4k0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
