\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Economists Size Up Risks Of Iran War --- Experts in a WSJ survey revise their forecasts on inflation, near-term growth and job creation as headwinds including higher oil prices threaten to persist\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Santilli and Anthony DeBarros \par \uc2 568 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The war in Iran has economists downshifting their outlook for the rest of the year, with the consensus in The Wall Street Journal's latest quarterly survey pointing to higher inflation, slower near-term growth and weaker job creation.\par \par \uc2 The following charts break down economists' forecasts for key indicators and show how the panel's 2025 consensus compares with actual outcomes. The latest survey, conducted April 3-9, reflects average forecasts from 68 economists. Not all respondents answered every question.\par \par \uc2 One of the key headwinds economists expect this year is higher oil prices that threaten to persist even after the war ends.\par \par \uc2 Economists placed the probability of a recession in the next 12 months at 33%, which is up from 27% in the January survey. However, they acknowledged that persistently high oil prices could push that likelihood above 50%.\par \par \uc2 Some forecasts indicate it would take West Texas Intermediate, the U.S. oil-price benchmark, rising to $125 per barrel or more and staying there for a year or longer, while others said prices above $100 per barrel for eight to 10 weeks would be enough.\par \par \uc2 Even excluding energy costs and food prices, the panel anticipates persistent price pressures.\par \par \uc2 The economists raised their consensus for year-end core inflation, based on the price index of personal-consumption expenditures, to 2.9% from 2.6% in January. The Federal Reserve targets 2% PCE inflation.\par \par \uc2 In the near term, economists still see reasonable economic growth. But they don't expect it to last. The panel increased its expectation for inflation-adjusted gross domestic product growth to 2.3% in the first quarter, but it cut forecasts for the second and third quarters compared with the January survey. For the year, they see growth at 2% on a fourth-quarter year-over-year basis, down from 2.2% in January.\par \par \uc2 That same weakness is expected to hit the labor market, with economists forecasting just 38,000 jobs added a month on average over the second and third quarters.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 How the Panel Fared in 2025\par \par \uc2 In January 2025, as President Trump was about to start his second term, economists forecast an uptick in inflation and modest growth for the year, with the Fed keeping interest rates higher than previously thought.\par \par \uc2 The 73 economists largely nailed growth and unemployment. Elsewhere, their consensus was close but slightly off -- core inflation came in hotter than expected, while interest rates were lower.\par \par \uc2 The panel's forecast for inflation-adjusted GDP in the fourth quarter of 2025 was 2.05% -- just shy of the actual value of 1.99%, while its forecast for 4.3% unemployment in December 2025 fell just below the actual reading of 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 Core PCE inflation, which excludes energy and food prices, came in at 2.9% year-over-year in the fourth quarter, above the 2.55% forecast. (The panel's consumer-price index forecast for December did match the actual 2.7%.)\par \par \uc2 Also, the economists' expectations that the midpoint of the range of the federal-funds rate range would end the year at 3.89% were higher than the 3.625% actual reading.\par \par \uc2 (See accompanying illustrations -- WSJ April 16, 2026)\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich309\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4635
