\par\par \b \uc2 Snap to Cut Staff 16% in Profit Push --- Parent of chat app to eliminate around 1,000 jobs as it faces investor pressure\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gareth Vipers \par \uc2 360 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Snap said it plans to cut around 1,000 jobs, roughly 16% of its full-time workforce, as the social-media company faces pressure from investors over its struggles for profitability.\par \par \uc2 In a memo to employees Wednesday, Snap Chief Executive \uc2 Evan Spiegel\uc2  said the cuts were necessary to enable the company to increase efficiency and pursue profitable growth. Snap shares rose nearly 8% in Wednesday's trading.\par \par \uc2 "Last fall, I described Snap as facing a crucible moment, requiring a new way of working that is faster and more efficient, while pivoting towards profitable growth," Spiegel wrote, adding that he expected the cuts to help reduce the company's annualized cost base by more than $500 million by the second half of 2026.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the cuts, Spiegel said the company would close more than 300 open positions.\par \par \uc2 Last month, activist investor \uc2 Irenic Capital Management\uc2 , which has built a stake of approximately 2.5% in Snap, urged the company's leadership to undertake a series of measures aimed at improving its valuation. Among the proposals were workforce reductions and the closure or potential spinoff of its Specs unit. Snap's stock is down more than 30% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In its letter, Irenic said that \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  should rationalize its cost structure, saying that the company had overhired. The investor pointed to recent layoffs at companies including \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  and Block as examples of efforts to boost efficiency.\par \par \uc2 It is the latest round of layoffs for the Santa Monica, Calif.-based company. In 2022, Snap cut roughly 20% of its employees amid slowing growth in advertising revenue, followed by an additional 10% global reduction in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Snap said it now estimates that its total revenue increased by 12% to reach $1.53 billion in the first quarter, according to revised investor materials. Adjusted earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization are estimated to be around $233 million for the period, the company said.\par \par \uc2 The company will hold its next earnings update on May 6.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260416em4g00020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
