\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Ad Firms, \uc2 FTC\uc2  Settle Boycott Probe\b0\par\par\uc2 By Suzanne Vranica \par \uc2 575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  and a coalition of eight states have reached a proposed settlement with advertising companies to resolve a probe into whether the firms violated antitrust laws by coordinating boycotts against platforms, including \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's X.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  began its inquiry last year, seeking information from the world's largest ad companies, including \uc2 Publicis Groupe\uc2 , \uc2 WPP\uc2  and \uc2 Dentsu\uc2 . The government's probe focused on whether the companies, along with various groups that help brands keep their ads away from controversial content, acted anticompetitively by coordinating to stop spending advertising money on specific online platforms.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal reported earlier this month that the \uc2 FTC\uc2  and ad firms were in settlement talks.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  alleged in a complaint filed Wednesday in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of Texas that the ad giants worked with third-party groups to establish ways to keep their ads away from misinformation or objectionable content.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2 's complaint said those entities, such as NewsGuard and the Global Disinformation Index, weaponized such designations to systematically demonetize disfavored political viewpoints -- particularly conservative news and commentary sites.\par \par \uc2 Brand-safety agreements "distorted the marketplace of ideas by discriminating against speech and ideas," said \uc2 FTC\uc2  Chairman Andrew N. Ferguson.\par \par \uc2 Also Wednesday, the parties filed a request for the judge to review the proposed settlement. \uc2 Dentsu\uc2 , \uc2 Publicis Groupe\uc2  and \uc2 WPP\uc2  have agreed to a proposed consent order that, if approved by the judge, would restrict ad placements based on biased and politically motivated criteria, the \uc2 FTC\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 WPP\uc2  said it had reached an agreement without admitting or denying wrongdoing. The company said the deal "reflects our existing and ongoing commitment to provide our clients with unbiased advice as they decide where to place their media."\par \par \uc2 Dentsu\uc2  said: "Our dedication to delivering value and maintaining the highest standards of compliance is unchanged."\par \par \uc2 Publicis\uc2  didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Over the past decade, "brand safety" -- the practice of ensuring ads don't appear next to inappropriate or controversial material -- has become a significant focus of the ad industry. So have questions about what makes for inappropriate content, or how damaging proximity to that material actually is for brands.\par \par \uc2 "Fully informed transactions between buyers and sellers are a key tenet of a free market," said a spokesperson for the Global Disinformation Index. "Advertisers and their agents have a free-speech right to choose where they want to place their advertisements."\par \par \uc2 "We don't work with the ad agencies signing the consent order, and our ratings are based on nonpartisan journalistic standards, not political orientation," said NewsGuard co-founder Gordon Crovitz. "We don't believe the \uc2 FTC\uc2  should be the arbiter of political bias or seek to censor our work."\par \par \uc2 The proposed settlement comes almost a year after the \uc2 FTC\uc2  reached a similar agreement with \uc2 Omnicom Group\uc2  as part of its $13.5 billion acquisition of rival \uc2 Interpublic Group\uc2 . That agreement barred the ad companies from coordinating with others to direct advertising away from media publishers based on the publishers' political or ideological viewpoints.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after Musk acquired \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in late 2022, many major advertisers paused their ads on the site, partly because of content-moderation concerns. In 2024, X sued an ad trade group and several big companies, claiming the group illegally boycotted the platform and violated antitrust laws.\par \par \uc2 A federal court dismissed the lawsuit last month.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260416em4g00012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
