\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Why Growth Without Jobs Might Be Fine --- Puzzled experts seize on productivity gains as economy expands amid employment rut\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harriet Torry \par \uc2 920 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For the past year, two indicators have told opposite stories of the economy's health: The economy itself grew in 2025, but despite a solid March, jobs didn't.\par \par \uc2 This puzzle could have an encouraging explanation: Productivity, which means workers are producing goods and services more efficiently, has revived at just the right time.\par \par \uc2 There are two main ways to expand the economy. The first is to add workers. This, though, has become harder since President Trump has brought most immigration to a halt, while an aging workforce and a decadeslong drop in birthrates have sapped growth in the native-born labor force. That leaves the second channel: Make the workers you have more productive.\par \par \uc2 "With labor-force growth slowing to a crawl, productivity is no longer just one of the engines of growth -- it's close to the only engine left," said \uc2 Erik Brynjolfsson\uc2 , director of the Stanford Digital Economy Lab.\par \par \uc2 And luckily, that engine is firing. According to the Labor Department, output per hour in the nonfarm business sector rose 2.1% last year. Growth has also averaged 2.1% over the past six years, a pickup from the 1.5% a year average from 2007 to 2019, and stronger than other Western economies.\par \par \uc2 This comes amid a falloff, unprecedented in recent history, in labor-force and job growth. In the year through March, immigration crackdowns and continuing, large-scale retirements among the baby-boomer generation mean the labor force -- people working or looking for a job -- declined by 554,000.\par \par \uc2 Outside of the pandemic, the last drop of that magnitude occurred in 2013 as baby boomers retired in large numbers. The longer-term outlook remains bleak, with the U.S. fertility rate at a record low.\par \par \uc2 With fewer new entrants to the labor force, the number of new jobs needed to keep the unemployment rate stable, called the break-even rate, has fallen, which helps explain why the jobless rate hasn't risen much the past year as job creation flatlined at under 22,000 a month on average, the lowest outside a recession since the year through March 2003.\par \par \uc2 The monthly break-even rate was much higher in the 1970s at 185,000 jobs, as women and baby boomers poured into the labor force. It dropped to 50,000 in late 2020 during the pandemic. This year, slower immigration means "the break-even pace could fall to nearly zero, requiring less than 10,000 new jobs a month in 2026," a recent paper from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  said. It estimated there might not be any growth in the labor force this year due to slower immigration and worker retirements. "Any growth in potential GDP this year would then need to come from productivity growth," the paper said. "This would represent a significant departure in the composition of economic growth from recent history."\par \par \uc2 These are uncharted waters.\par \par \uc2 "Zero job growth just doesn't map into any kind of stability in terms of employment and whatnot," Federal Reserve governor \uc2 Christopher Waller\uc2  said at an economics conference in February. "This would be the first time in my career, my life, that I saw an economy growing like this and zero job growth."\par \par \uc2 Ordinarily, a stagnant labor force would weigh on economic growth. Japan, for example, has grown just 0.7% a year since 2000, in great part because of a shrinking population and flat labor force.\par \par \uc2 Productivity growth is volatile and often driven by short-lived cyclical factors. The recent acceleration, though, might be durable. Economists say the Covid lockdowns and worker shortages that followed forced many companies to speed up automation. A hot labor market and shift to remote-work policies allowed greater numbers of workers to move into jobs they performed more productively.\par \par \uc2 The number of workers quitting jobs each month has fallen to a six-year low, and the longer workers stay in jobs, the more productive they tend to become.\par \par \uc2 "Coming out of Covid, the labor force saw a spate of dynamism that it hadn't seen in a long time," said Chad Syverson, professor of economics at the \uc2 University of Chicago Booth School of Business\uc2 . "People shuffled into jobs that were better for them," he said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Trying to Gauge AI's Effect\par \par \uc2 Economists say it is too early to determine the role of artificial intelligence on workers' increased job productivity, but they expect it is helping.\par \par \uc2 While AI has been around for decades, it was turbocharged by the 2022 release of ChatGPT, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's chatbot. "If you squint, you could tell yourself that story in the data," said Chad Syverson, professor of economics at the \uc2 University of Chicago Booth School of Business\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, some forecasters are building higher productivity into long-term growth expectations. In March, \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials raised their long-term growth-rate estimate to 2% from 1.8%. \uc2 Kevin Warsh\uc2 , nominated by Trump to be the next Fed chair, has said faster productivity growth due to AI will hold down cost pressures, enabling the Fed to lower interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Erik Brynjolfsson, director of the Stanford Digital Economy Lab, expects the gains from AI will be meaningful, but not immediate.\par \par \uc2 "The biggest gains from a powerful general-purpose technology usually arrive only after firms invest in the complements: reorganizing workflows, retraining workers, redesigning processes and building the intangible capital needed to use the technology effectively," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260414em4e0000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
