\par\par \b \uc2 Restaurants Are Finding It Hard to Hire Dishwashers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 668 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GENEVA, Ill. -- Wielding a faucet hose, Beto "Rodrigo" Mejia Fermine blasted the remains of avocado toast and turkey gravy off stacks of plates hurriedly delivered by servers. Nearby, Luciano Vera sprayed bacon grease from cooking trays before loading them into an industrial dishwasher.\par \par \uc2 Thirty minutes into their dishwashing shifts at a First Watch restaurant west of Chicago, the two had cleaned dozens of plates, utensils, cutting boards and cups, making them ready to be used again. It was 9:30 a.m., and the brunch rush had hours to go.\par \par \uc2 More than 12 million people work in the U.S. restaurant and bar industry. Dishwashers occupy one of the humblest rungs, but they are among the most badly needed. Restaurant operators advertised tens of thousands of dishwashing jobs last year, making them one of the industry's most-sought positions, according to labor market analytics firm Lightcast.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration's tougher immigration enforcement is making that harder in the U.S. restaurant industry, where foreign-born workers make up about 20% of jobs. Young people also are more reluctant to take the longtime starting point to the working world, industry officials said.\par \par \uc2 That adds pressure for industry leaders like \uc2 Chris Tomasso\uc2 , chief executive of the 630-location \uc2 First Watch Restaurant Group\uc2 , who said one of his top worries is having enough people like Fermine and Vera. "That's a difficult position, and a critical position," he said.\par \par \uc2 Rick Cardenas\uc2 , CEO of Olive Garden parent \uc2 Darden Restaurants\uc2 , said dishwashers are his top employment concern. At New York City-based \uc2 Union Square Hospitality Group\uc2 , which operates Union Square Cafe and Gramercy Tavern, CEO \uc2 Chip Wade\uc2  agrees, with the company providing meals and family discounts to help keep its dishwashers. Sushi chain \uc2 Kura Sushi\uc2  is going as far as to import robotic dishwashers from Japan for $15,000 each to ease the strain.\par \par \uc2 Dishwashers' pay averages around $32,500 a year, ranking in the bottom third of restaurant jobs, according to jobs platform Indeed. They are also essential workhorses, often the last to leave at night, after mopping floors and taking out the trash.\par \par \uc2 Restaurant and other related service workers quit at the highest rates of any industry tracked by the federal government on average. Market-research firm Black Box Intelligence estimated that replacing an hourly restaurant worker costs $2,700, up from $2,300 in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Jayme Patton has spent five years washing dishes at various restaurants in the Tucson, Ariz., area. At times co-workers have thrown dishes, managers have shouted at him, and he has been left to clean all the plates and kitchen equipment on his own after closing.\par \par \uc2 "Dishwashing is not a profession I would ever recommend for someone who has any other options," he said.\par \par \uc2 First Watch says its restaurants have lower turnover than the industry average, and churn fell last year. But turnover is higher among the chain's dishwashers than other positions on average, said Tomasso, the CEO.\par \par \uc2 About 5% of First Watch's 1,557 dishwashers have been with the chain for more than five years. The company said it provides growth plans for hourly employees to help them move up.\par \par \uc2 Fifty-four percent of sit-down restaurants surveyed by the \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2  said they had fewer-than-average applicants for kitchen-support positions last year. Michelle Korsmo, the group's president, said the Trump administration's stepped-up immigration enforcement isn't helping. "Even for operators that do E-Verify, and do all the things, there is a real unrest in terms of making sure everyone is safe and not accidentally getting caught up in something," Korsmo said.\par \par \uc2 Restaurant trade groups have been pushing the White House and Congress to create a visa program for lower-skilled immigrant workers. Korsmo said she has appealed to lawmakers' own restaurant-industry histories, like Senate Majority Leader \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , whose first job was as a busser at a South Dakota restaurant.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260414em4e0000s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
