\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Jobs Market Confounds Prognosticators --- Wall Street experts have had big misses as they adjust to more volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman \par \uc2 779 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If it feels like economists have been slumping lately, it's not just your imagination. With three chances so far in 2026 to project monthly job creation, Wall Street's soothsayers have struck out.\par \par \uc2 To be fair, they have been thrown some major curveballs. In a fast-changing labor market, the Trump administration's immigration crackdown has cut population growth, downshifting job creation to a trend that can land on either side of zero in any given month. Strikes and bad weather have caused labor-market hiccups this year. And technical changes have made official calculations less predictable than they used to be.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think forecasting the headline labor market numbers has been this challenging at any point of my career," said Jonathan Pingle, a \uc2 UBS\uc2  economist.\par \par \uc2 In January, forecasters' consensus vastly undershot the net gain of 130,000 jobs that the Labor Department initially reported. Then in February, with economists expecting further steady gains, they were wrong-footed by sharp job losses, which partially reflected bad weather and a big healthcare strike. March brought another whiff, when forecasters were counting on moderate job gains and wound up blindsided by a blowout increase.\par \par \uc2 This yields a yawning gap of 112,000 monthly jobs, on average, between what the Labor Department first reported and analysts' projections so far this year. The Wall Street Journal analyzed the projections it collects each month against the first Labor Department jobs reports, before revisions that alter numbers later.\par \par \uc2 Pingle's \uc2 UBS\uc2  team did quite a bit better than most on the March data, estimating that 100,000 new jobs were created, versus the Wall Street consensus of 59,000. But he still underestimated the actual tally by some 78,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 Still, this doesn't mean Wall Street has suddenly lost the plot. Analysts averaged a monthly miss of roughly 38,100 jobs in 2025, the data show, better than average misses nearing 75,000 a month a year before.\par \par \uc2 The challenge increases when the economy is changing rapidly. In 2020, when companies were shedding jobs in an escalating pandemic, projections were often sharply off.\par \par \uc2 Nailing economic forecasts can be high-stakes for financial-industry economists. When official government numbers land, stock and bond prices instantly adjust to new clues about not only how the economy is doing, but how the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is likely to respond.\par \par \uc2 Making your mark in this game is a subtle art, said Robert Barbera, a \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2  economics professor who spent decades making predictions on Wall Street. Publishing a forecast that differs significantly from competitors' amounts to throwing your weight behind a strong trading recommendation, so many economists hesitate to go out on a limb.\par \par \uc2 "If I go out there and say that the next three payroll numbers are going to be minus-100,000, I'm effectively saying, you've got to own the 10-year, or you're stupid," Barbera said, referring to 10-year Treasury notes, which often rally as the economy slows. It is often more comfortable to make moderate forecasts projecting that recent trends will continue, even if that risks the occasional big miss, he said.\par \par \uc2 The past few months have brought the type of volatility that makes those misses more common. Healthcare employment seesawed, for instance.\par \par \uc2 Perceptive forecasters are paid to factor in developments like that. But the winter's nasty weather was more of a wild card, and contributed to significantly bigger month-to-month swings than usual in February and March, according to a model published by economists at the \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 More obscure -- yet significant -- challenges are also making analysts' jobs harder.\par \par \uc2 The government surveys more than 100,000 employers each month to understand how much hiring and firing is happening. Since the pandemic, far fewer companies have been responding on time, making it less likely that those that do accurately reflect what is happening.\par \par \uc2 What's more, jobs created or destroyed when companies are founded or go out of business have always been a big blind spot, so the Labor Department's stats arm, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , relies on a model for estimates.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  fine-tuned this model in January as part of an effort to reduce big revisions that often come later. But that change has made the initially reported numbers less predictable for now.\par \par \uc2 In January and February combined, the swings attributable to this "birth-death model" were about 100,000 jobs more than usual, economist Abiel Reinhart of \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 's economics team has estimated.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich313\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal4695
