\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Why More Are Leaving the U.S. Job Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeanne Whalen \par \uc2 513 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. labor market bounced back last month with healthy job growth and a decline in unemployment. But another trend also came into focus: the continuing fall in labor-force participation.\par \par \uc2 The share of the working-age population that is either working or looking for work -- known as the labor-force participation rate -- edged down to 61.9% in March, its lowest level since 1977, outside of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The rate has been gradually falling since the early 2000s, largely due to the aging population. It nosedived in the early months of the pandemic, then rebounded and grew for a while before resuming its downward slide in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The continued aging of the population and the Trump administration's immigration crackdown have helped drive the fall in recent months, economists said. The rate matters because it helps set the pace of economic growth. The economy grows either because more workers join it or because each worker produces more.\par \par \uc2 "A lower labor force participation rate means slower long-run economic growth," said \uc2 Gus Faucher\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 PNC Financial Services Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fortunately, U.S. productivity growth has been above historical averages for the past several years, said \uc2 Greg Daco\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 EY\uc2 -Parthenon. "That has essentially partially offset the slowdown in the growth of the labor force," he said. "But there's an open question as to how much more productivity growth we'll get in the coming years," he added.\par \par \uc2 The participation rate has been under pressure since the baby boomers started retiring in the early 2000s, a trend that has continued in recent months as younger boomers reach retirement age. Early retirement among workers 55 and older has been another factor. The pandemic prompted many in this demographic to leave the workforce before 65, and the trend has continued. The labor-force participation rate for Americans 55 and over has fallen from 40.2% in January 2020 to 37.2% last month, the lowest in more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 The administration's immigration policies have been another drag on the participation rate. Lower levels of immigration, along with deportations, have cut off a pipeline of often young residents who come to the country specifically to work.\par \par \uc2 "With immigration declining and fewer people entering, we have aged our workforce in a more rapid way," said Laura Ullrich, an economist at the job-search site Indeed.\par \par \uc2 Over time, a lower participation rate could spell more trouble for the U.S. economy "because we are also at some point going to be experiencing declines in population," Ullrich said. A smaller pool of potential workers, combined with lower participation rates within that pool, could lead to labor shortages in some areas, she said.\par \par \uc2 On a positive note, the participation rate of people ages 25 to 54 has held near multidecade highs -- evidence the overall decline in participation is tied more to the aging population and immigration shock than to people struggling to find work and giving up, economists say.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich466\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6990
