\par\par \b \uc2 Dimon Spotlights Risks for \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  And World\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alexander Saeedy \par \uc2 1,190 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  has a lot on his mind.\par \par \uc2 There's the war in Iran. Americans' loss of trust in the government. The \uc2 European Union\uc2 's future. Intensifying competition from startups and other banks, and artificial intelligence's transformation of the workforce.\par \par \uc2 In his annual letter to shareholders, the \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  chief executive laid out a range of risks he sees for the nation's biggest bank as well as the world. Dimon warned that the war in Iran could push up inflation and drag down financial markets even further if interest rates start to rise.\par \par \uc2 Dimon's letter to shareholders has become a tradition since he became chief executive in the 2000s. Over the years, he has moved beyond focusing on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's performance to sharing broader geopolitical and socioeconomic commentary, often warning of what he sees as underappreciated risks to the economy and world.\par \par \uc2 Dimon also said the outcome of conflicts between the world's great powers, including the wars in Ukraine and Iran, is more important than the financial or economic impacts they might bring.\par \par \uc2 "We should not turn a blind eye to the role the current regime in Iran has played in fostering terrorism and killing thousands of people, including Americans and many of its own citizens, over many years," Dimon said. "That threat must be addressed in an appropriate manner."\par \par \uc2 He offered advice on how the \uc2 European Union\uc2  should reform, gave suggestions on how to improve the U.S. public education system and claimed that higher taxes had caused an exodus of people from cities like New York.\par \par \uc2 He said that while the city remains \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's headquarters, the bank has shrunk its head count there and warned that no city had a "divine right to success."\par \par \uc2 Left unmentioned in this year's letter was a recent lawsuit brought by President Trump against \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  and Dimon for closing his bank accounts after the Jan. 6, 2021, riot at the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Dimon said he supported the deregulatory push Trump has fostered since he took office, adding that \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  planned to help the White House achieve its broader policy goals by supporting industries critical to the country's military and economic security.\par \par \uc2 "JPMorganChase is well-positioned to do its part," Dimon said.\par \par \uc2 The executive has long prognosticated about risks he sees to the economy, though they often don't come to pass. Dimon has said he looks at a range of possible outcomes, and he isn't predicting that they will happen.\par \par \uc2 Here are the risks Dimon called out in his latest letter:\par \par \uc2 Inflation\par \par \uc2 Since the U.S. and Israel began bombing Iran in February, oil prices have skyrocketed as Tehran has halted passage of most ships through the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 Dimon said there is a risk of more oil and commodity price shocks in the months ahead, which could lead to prolonged inflation and higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "The skunk at the party -- and it could happen in 2026 -- would be inflation slowly going up," Dimon wrote. "This alone could cause interest rates to rise and asset prices to drop."\par \par \uc2 Still, Dimon said the outcome of conflicts between the world's great powers is more important than the financial impacts that they may bring.\par \par \uc2 More competition\par \par \uc2 Dimon also has reason to worry about rivals chipping away at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's business.\par \par \uc2 In addition to stepped up competition from other banks and regional lenders, he noted \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  faced a growing set of nontraditional rivals in payments and digital banking.\par \par \uc2 And he noted a "whole new set of competitors is emerging based on blockchain," including stablecoins. "I'm not going to mention all their names, but you can imagine that we study and track over a hundred of them," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 There was plenty about Wall Street in this year's letter, too.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  is right in the middle of the reckoning in private credit, and Dimon predicted that most types of high-risk credit would take a bigger-than-expected hit in a downturn as underwriting standards among many lenders have deteriorated.\par \par \uc2 He also argued that the push from private credit funds to sell investments to retail clients required "greater transparency" and "higher standards" than was currently the case.\par \par \uc2 "Not everyone providing credit is necessarily good at it," Dimon said. "There are many players who are late to this game, and it should be expected that some credit providers will do a far worse job than others."\par \par \uc2 He also took a jab at the private-equity industry, which has been the subject of criticism in past missives to his shareholders.\par \par \uc2 "With stock markets at all-time highs in recent months, it is a little surprising that private-equity firms, which own close to 13,000 companies, have not taken greater advantage of healthy markets to take their companies public," he said. "It's hard to imagine what will happen if and when we have an extended bear market."\par \par \uc2 Loss of trust\par \par \uc2 Dimon argued that Americans are losing trust in government because of wasteful spending and high taxes in many cities and states.\par \par \uc2 He also noted that people are heading for the exits from those places, arguing that no city has a "divine right to success."\par \par \uc2 He also worried that economic growth isn't being equally spread, keeping the "American dream" out of reach for millions. Dimon proposed a number of regulatory fixes that would make it easier to build affordable housing and start businesses, all of which would help the economy grow. "Growth is part of the solution to almost all of our problems," he said.\par \par \uc2 AI transformation\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan has been embracing artificial intelligence, and Dimon has acknowledged the technology will influence how the bank thinks about staffing needs among its 300,000-plus employees.\par \par \uc2 "AI will affect virtually every function, application and process in the company," Dimon wrote. It "will definitely eliminate some jobs, while it enhances others."\par \par \uc2 A big concern Dimon highlighted is how the government should help prepare society for the workforce transformation AI is poised to bring. "There is a possibility that AI deployment will move faster than workforce adaptation to new job creation," he said. "Business and government can do many things to properly incent retraining, income assistance, reskilling, early retirement and relocation for those whose jobs might be adversely impacted by AI."\par \par \uc2 Weak allies\par \par \uc2 Dimon expressed worries about the future of the European Union.\par \par \uc2 He has long said that he's worried about the fate of the Western world, and he put plenty of blame on Europe's inability to reform and present a united front against world powers like China and Russia. "Europe is entering a decisive decade, and it is unable to act," he said.\par \par \uc2 He cited a range of problems for the EU, including internal trade barriers and expensive social programs. But he praised how EU countries have boosted spending on their militaries and said the continent still had time to turn things around.\par \par \uc2 "While America and Europe do have real differences, we believe that a stronger Europe, militarily and economically, is in America's self-interest," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260407em470001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
