\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Report Cites Long Setbacks for AI-Displaced Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Te-Ping Chen \par \uc2 696 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The rise of artificial intelligence has quickly sparked worries of job losses in America's office cubicles. Whether this happens is a big question, but a new \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  report analyzing past technology waves warns AI-displaced workers face potentially steep economic pain.\par \par \uc2 The report, released Monday, draws on four decades of federal data and tracks the lives of more than 20,000 Americans born between the 1950s and 1980s. Compared with workers who lost jobs in more stable occupations, Goldman's researchers said that displaced workers in jobs hit by technological shifts -- such as telephone operators and typists -- suffered both short- and long-term economic impacts.\par \par \uc2 "AI-driven displacement could impose lasting costs on affected workers, worsening labor market outcomes for several years," authors Pierfrancesco Mei and Jessica Rindels wrote.\par \par \uc2 In particular, their data show, workers who lose jobs in fields hit by automation take a month longer to find new jobs compared with workers displaced from other fields. These automation-vulnerable workers also suffer real earning losses of 3% after landing a new job, while their peers saw a negligible impact.\par \par \uc2 In the decade after losing a job in a technologically disrupted field, the Goldman authors found, workers saw their real earnings grow nearly 10 percentage points less than those who never suffered any job loss, and 5 percentage points less than workers displaced in other industries. One likely reason, they said, is that tech-displaced workers are hit by "occupational downgrading" when their skills become less valuable.\par \par \uc2 Economic downturns heighten the bad effects. Workers who lose jobs in occupations that are being automated during a recession tend to face an additional three weeks of unemployment and steeper likelihood of later unemployment, the new report said. Still, the authors note that worker retraining programs can help insulate the unemployed from wage losses.\par \par \uc2 The jury remains very much out on whether AI will prove so disruptive. Some economists think the technology will boost fortunes for workers by complementing their skills and allowing them to work more productively. Many economists also believe that even if AI causes substantial job losses, it is likely to fuel other kinds of job growth.\par \par \uc2 A recent survey of 750 U.S. chief financial officers found essentially no negative employment effect from AI last year, with most believing the technology will prompt only minor reductions in overall staffing this year. Another analysis of U.S. job postings by researchers at the \uc2 Federal Reserve Board\uc2  found no indication that job postings had fallen for industries or companies using more AI.\par \par \uc2 Another recent Goldman report suggests a sharper impact: It estimated AI is causing a net cut of about 16,000 jobs a month in the U.S., with an unquantified number of offsetting hires to build data centers, or due to new demand for workers caused by AI productivity or income gains. Over the next decade, Goldman's global economics team has estimated that AI could lead to job losses for 6% to 7% of U.S. workers while increasing the unemployment rate by up to half a percentage point.\par \par \uc2 Displacement can be especially destabilizing for early career workers between age 25 and 35, Goldman found. They are less likely to marry than their never-displaced peers and also face a higher likelihood of delayed life milestones, such as homeownership.\par \par \uc2 Young graduates have faced a particularly chilly job market in recent years as interest rates rose and as companies pulled back on a pandemic-era hiring spree. Some young Americans are now pivoting their careers to try to seek paths that seem less likely to end up derailed by automation.\par \par \uc2 Still, the new Goldman report finds that younger and college-educated workers are among the best-positioned to weather AI disruptions. Among such workers who lost jobs in technology-disrupted occupations, the authors found, the cumulative hit to their earnings was half as large as that of their peers.\par \par \uc2 "Contrary to current concerns that the costs of AI will fall especially hard on new graduates, younger workers have actually been able to adjust more flexibly," they wrote.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260407em470000z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
