\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Job Market Keeps Humming as It Absorbs Shocks, but Outlook Is Murky\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Timiraos \par \uc2 761 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The March jobs report offered a reminder of why so many economists have been reluctant to bet against the U.S. labor market: Even after four years of shocks, it keeps finding its footing.\par \par \uc2 The question now is whether that can last.\par \par \uc2 Over the last few years, the job market has weathered the most aggressive rate-hiking cycle in decades, a regional banking crisis and a tariff shock. Each time, it bent but didn't break. The latest jolt to energy prices and supply chains from the Iran war will again test the limits of that resilience.\par \par \uc2 Employers added 178,000 jobs in March, reversing a larger-than-previously-reported decline of 133,000 in February. The unemployment rate also unwound February's increase, ticking down to 4.3%. Some details were less encouraging. Wage growth for rank-and-file workers slowed to its weakest year-over-year pace since the pandemic reopening five years ago.\par \par \uc2 Averaging the two volatile months together gives a clearer picture of the underlying trend and showed monthly gains of just 22,500 jobs. While that pace would have been alarming two years ago, the labor market has held together in large part because a sharp drop in immigration and an increase in retirees means economists now think it needs fewer new jobs, on net, than it used to just to stand still.\par \par \uc2 Coming into 2026, economists had hoped the job market was done slowing. March's solid headline number might have been a glimpse of that. But after the closure of the Strait of Hormuz upended global energy supply chains, "nobody's talking about reacceleration now," said Guy Berger, a labor economist.\par \par \uc2 The labor market had already been adjusting to a significant change in immigration policy that has reduced the pool of potential workers. Firms have been slow to hire but reluctant to fire. It's a job market consistent with full employment but with very low growth and much less dynamism, leaving little cushion.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials have been grappling with what it would mean if the economy needs far fewer jobs to keep the unemployment rate steady. "Conveying that a zero-job-growth economy is consistent with full employment is not easy," \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Mary Daly\uc2  said in a blog post Friday. The limited inflow of new workers means the economy's speed limit is lower, she said, and the risk of a miscalculation -- holding interest rates either too low or too high -- is greater.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty from the war has already shifted how Fed officials talk about the path of interest rates. Before the conflict, many policymakers still expected to resume cutting rates this year. Now, more have indicated the Fed could be on hold indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 The optimistic case is that the war and the resulting supply-chain disruptions don't last much longer, limiting the damage to hiring. The pessimistic case is that a prolonged conflict sends price shocks rippling through fertilizers, industrial chemicals and semiconductor production. Higher costs for employers and consumers could squeeze the spending that supports new hiring.\par \par \uc2 Unlike the energy shock from Russia's invasion of Ukraine in 2022, consumers have already drawn down savings buffers, and wage growth is decelerating. That means households have less room to absorb higher prices without cutting back on spending. If they do cut back, businesses that depend on that spending could reduce hours and head count.\par \par \uc2 The bottom 60% of earners keep spending as long as they're employed because so much of their budget goes to necessities, said \uc2 Nathan Sheets\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . "What would break them would be if the labor market softened significantly," he said.\par \par \uc2 Already, the costs are starting to add up. The fiscal boost that was supposed to carry growth this spring -- and with it, support for hiring -- is in a race against the gas pump. Economists at the \uc2 St. Louis Fed\uc2  estimate the increase in fuel prices over the past month could cost consumers the equivalent of 10% to 50% of President Trump's tax relief enacted last year for every quarter that gas prices stay around current levels.\par \par \uc2 Every dollar that goes into the gas tank is a dollar that doesn't go to the restaurants, retailers and service businesses that account for the bulk of American employment. Rising bond yields have pushed mortgage rates back toward 6.5% from 6%, dimming prospects for a lift to housing that could support construction jobs.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260406em4600014\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
