\par\par \b \uc2 Striking Beef Plant Staff Return to Work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Thomas \par \uc2 240 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of striking beef-plant workers in Colorado agreed to return to work, ending a three-week strike at a slaughter plant owned by JBS, the world's largest meatpacker.\par \par \uc2 The union representing 3,800 striking workers at the Greeley, Colo., beef plant said Saturday that JBS has agreed to a new round of contract negotiations, with talks set to resume April 9 and 10. Workers will report for their shifts starting Monday.\par \par \uc2 JBS's Greeley plant can slaughter about 6,000 cattle a day, representing roughly 5% of U.S. beef-processing capacity. The strike began on March 16.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers International Union\uc2  last year agreed to a new long-term labor contract covering about 26,000 JBS workers across more than a dozen U.S. facilities. It also provided meatpacking workers with a pension plan for the first time in decades.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UFCW\uc2  local in Greeley opted out of the deal, saying JBS's wage increases don't keep pace with inflation. "Workers remain united and will continue to fight," \uc2 Kim Cordova\uc2 , the local's president, said Saturday.\par \par \uc2 JBS executives have said the company's proposals to the union were fair and remain on the table. A JBS spokeswoman said Saturday that the decision by the union to return to work comes without any new agreement or change to the company's original offer.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260406em460001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
