\par\par \b \uc2 Hiring Posts Big Surprise Rebound In March --- Unemployment rate declined to 4.3%; gains are attributed to healthcare sector\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman \par \uc2 837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. added 178,000 jobs in March, the Labor Department said Friday, a resilient rebound that blew past expectations and defied concerns about a possible downturn.\par \par \uc2 March's job gains marked the best month for growth in more than a year and were far better than February, when the U.S. lost more than 130,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 Last month's numbers also beat the gain of 59,000 jobs that economists polled by The Wall Street Journal had expected to see.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate fell to 4.3%, from February's 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 The decline in the unemployment rate came with an asterisk: The labor force shrank by nearly 400,000 people, meaning fewer people were counted as unemployed. The share of people working or looking for work slipped to 61.9%, its lowest level since the fall of 2021.\par \par \uc2 "We should all be rooting for a higher participation rate," said Henry McVey, head of global macro and asset allocation at private-equity firm \uc2 KKR\uc2 . "You're in a solid, but not spectacular, growth environment."\par \par \uc2 Investors took the latest jobs numbers in stride, in part because many markets including stocks in the U.S. and Europe were closed for Good Friday. Treasury yields edged higher as the healthier labor report was viewed as more likely to spur inflation and less likely to convince the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates. The benchmark 10-year Treasury yield ended the day slightly above 4.34%, up from 4.31% Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The February jobs number, meanwhile, was worse than previously reported. The Labor Department said that the economy lost 133,000 jobs in February, after previously estimating a loss of 92,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 "I always tell people to look not just at the latest month, but to ask, what's been the picture for the past three or four months?" said \uc2 Gus Faucher\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 PNC Financial Services\uc2 . "When you look at the broader picture, we're adding jobs at a slower pace than a couple years ago, but we are adding jobs, and it's enough to keep the unemployment rate steady."\par \par \uc2 All told, the economy has created an average of 15,000 jobs each month over the past half year. That is a big downshift from the same period a year earlier, when the U.S. was adding 78,000 jobs on average each month.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has lost jobs in five of the past 12 months.\par \par \uc2 The job gains were driven by a big rebound for the healthcare and social-assistance sector. It added about 90,000 jobs in March.\par \par \uc2 That was a reversal from February, when a large West Coast strike sidelined tens of thousands of workers.\par \par \uc2 The rebound in jobs also looked as if it reflected an improvement in the weather from February, noted Jefferies economist Thomas Simons. That helped the leisure and hospitality and construction sectors.\par \par \uc2 Leisure and hospitality added 44,000 jobs. Construction added 26,000. Manufacturing employment rose by 15,000.\par \par \uc2 One of the few weak spots was the public sector. Federal-government payrolls lost 18,000 jobs, more than offsetting net hiring by local agencies.\par \par \uc2 Hiring has weathered a complicated set of crosscurrents over the past year. Job growth has downshifted, but Friday's data confirms that the labor market hasn't been derailed.\par \par \uc2 Last year, uncertainty about big changes to trade, immigration and tax policy put many companies into hold-steady mode, delaying big hiring and layoff decisions until the picture becomes clearer.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the Trump administration's immigration crackdown has reduced the number of available workers. So the economy no longer needs to add as many jobs to keep the same share of workers employed.\par \par \uc2 The war in Iran means the economy is now facing a new set of challenges -- and most economists think that the March jobs data arrive too early to see the effects. The sharp rise in oil prices raises costs and could yield more inflation, but it could also strain household budgets and drag down demand.\par \par \uc2 Because hiring plans are often made months in advance, the March numbers probably don't give a clear read on how these effects will play out.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said this past week that the Iran war raises the risk that the central bank might have to choose between fighting inflation and shoring up the labor market but that it isn't facing that trade-off yet. Friday's data rammed that point home.\par \par \uc2 The Fed was already planning to hold rates steady at its coming April 28-29 meeting because elevated oil prices and underlying inflation around 3% had closed the door to cuts regardless.\par \par \uc2 But the March jobs data relieve officials of the harder question looming behind that decision: whether a weakening labor market would eventually force them to consider cutting even with inflation uncomfortably high.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich488\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7320
