\par\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Expands Incentive-Pay Plan For Store Baristas\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 410 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is adding incentives for baristas to boost their service and sales game.\par \par \uc2 The coffee chain said Thursday that it will expand tipping for U.S. baristas when customers pay with credit or debit cards for mobile orders. Tipping currently is restricted to customers placing in-store and drive-through orders with cash or credit cards, or mobile transactions using \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  cards. Tipping will soon be offered when credit cards are used for mobile orders and scanning at the register.\par \par \uc2 The Seattle-based company also plans to provide bonuses to baristas whose stores meet performance goals, including targets for sales, operations and customer satisfaction. Store baristas and shift supervisors who meet the top level of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's internal ratings for their location can earn a $300 quarterly bonus, or $1,200 for a full year, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said the expanded tipping and new bonuses should increase baristas' compensation by an estimated 5% to 8% on top of their current pay, though that depends partly on how much customers choose to tip. The company aims to introduce the new benefits in its company-owned stores in July.\par \par \uc2 "This reward recognizes teams that work together to deliver excellence," \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  executives said in a company letter Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The moves come as \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is in the midst of a turnaround of its U.S. business, with Chief Executive \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2  aiming to improve service as a means of increasing sales.\par \par \uc2 It is also a delicate time between the world's largest coffee chain and some of its U.S. baristas. The company has yet to agree to terms of a contract with the Starbucks Workers United union representing roughly 600 of its 10,000 U.S. stores.\par \par \uc2 Some union-represented cafes shut down or limited service for periods during the winter to protest the lack of progress.\par \par \uc2 Union-represented baristas have been pushing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to agree to more pay and staffing.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United and the company last month agreed to return to the bargaining table.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations were expected to start this month, the first direct talks between both sides in more than a year.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said its baristas currently average $30 an hour in total pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 The company and union will need to bargain separately over extending the new bonus and tipping benefits to the roughly 5% of U.S. stores represented by Starbucks Workers United, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260403em430001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
