\par\par \b \uc2 Empty EV Parts Factories Create a New Rust Belt --- GM supplier \uc2 Magna\uc2  is stuck with a plant to build parts for electric pickups; 'the magnitude of uncertainty is unparalleled'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 1,946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ST. CLAIR, Mich. -- At first, North America's biggest auto-parts supplier was thrilled to snag the job of making enclosures for the batteries in \uc2 General Motors\uc2 ' new electric pickup. The contract was so big -- and promised to be for years to come -- that \uc2 Magna International\uc2  built a new factory in a Michigan cornfield.\par \par \uc2 Five years later, that million-square-foot plant is mostly empty and losing money, a casualty of America's messy breakup with electric vehicles. It is one of dozens of now desolate or sparsely used EV parts plants across the country.\par \par \uc2 Now the war in Iran has driven gas prices up so sharply that EV enthusiasts are daring to wonder whether U.S. car buyers are willing to give the vehicles another look. But \uc2 Magna\uc2  and its big Detroit customers are forging ahead with plans to roll back EV investments.\par \par \uc2 It can take years to pivot a factory and supply chain from one type of vehicle to another. And it would take four to six months of higher gas prices for most Americans to reconsider more fuel-efficient vehicles, said \uc2 Paul Jacobson\uc2 , GM's chief financial officer. "We certainly don't see it today," he said recently. GM said this week it would idle the Detroit factory where it builds the big electric trucks that \uc2 Magna\uc2  supplies, due to weak demand.\par \par \uc2 "The magnitude of uncertainty is unparalleled," said \uc2 Magna\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Swamy Kotagiri\uc2 , who joined the company as an engineer in 1999 and has run it since 2021. A \uc2 Magna\uc2  team recently walked through the St. Clair EV parts factory trying to figure out what equipment might be repurposed. It will take 18 to 24 months, the company figures, to find new customers and get enough production to be profitable again.\par \par \uc2 Shareholders have cheered the decisions by Detroit automakers to scrap their boldest EV dreams -- looking beyond $50 billion in charges tied to broken supplier contracts and wasted investments. Many of the hundreds of workers hired and then laid off by \uc2 Magna\uc2  in St. Clair have had other job opportunities with nearby factories looking for workers.\par \par \uc2 It is \uc2 Magna\uc2 , which has more than 300 factories around the globe and parts in nearly every car on the road today, that has been left holding the keys to a largely deserted St. Clair building that is bigger than 20 football fields. The company, based outside Toronto, needs to find a second life for the factory and the hulking rows of assembly-line robots it spent roughly $575 million setting up.\par \par \uc2 Answers aren't easy when the biggest automakers disagree over what Americans will be driving even a few years from now. Trump administration policies and tariffs have cost automakers billions and continue to sow uncertainty. Automakers no longer face the strict Biden-era fuel-economy rules that once spurred production of more electric models. Ford has killed its electric F-150 truck and is shifting to gas-electric hybrids and plug-in vehicles while it tries to crack the code on cheap EVs. GM is keeping its electric trucks even as demand craters, and CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  has made a case against hybrid vehicles.\par \par \uc2 A few years ago, \uc2 Magna\uc2  had plans to build an entirely new business unit around EV battery enclosures, one that Kotagiri predicted would eventually deliver $2.5 billion a year in revenue. In a recent interview, he said he still believes EVs will take hold in the U.S. as they have elsewhere in the world, but he's making no guess when that will happen.\par \par \uc2 Warfare in the Middle East has only ratcheted up the uncertainty. While a prolonged stretch of pricey gas could renew interest in EVs, it also could further slow overall sales of all vehicles if cash-crunched consumers respond by putting off new purchases. Last Thursday, research firm \uc2 J.D. Power\uc2  estimated that overall vehicle sales would fall more than 10% in March, compared with a year ago, with EVs losing market share.\par \par \uc2 The unpredictability is reverberating through the automotive industry's sprawling supply chain. Multinational companies such as \uc2 Magna\uc2 , Dana and \uc2 BorgWarner\uc2  slashed jobs and closed plants due to the EV pullback, while a string of smaller manufacturers shut down altogether. Last year, more than $20 billion in previously announced investments in EV and battery facilities were wiped out, according to Atlas Public Policy, which tracks clean-economy investments.\par \par \uc2 "They can't plan. It's just chaotic and hard to make any kind of a move," said Catherine Karol, a lawyer at Butzel, a Detroit law firm that represents auto-parts suppliers.\par \par \uc2 Smaller suppliers in particular have little recourse to recoup costs when automakers cancel a vehicle program and stop buying parts, Karol said. "They're basically SOL," she said, because a supplier typically absorbs the upfront cost of setting up an assembly line with the expectation of recouping it over time as parts are shipped.\par \par \uc2 In St. Clair, \uc2 Magna\uc2  has opened its facility to tours by other automakers and suppliers, hoping to lure new business to occupy empty swaths of the factory floor. Outside, freshly laid parking lots sit mostly empty while a single, shrunken shift of workers comes and goes. Local officials and business owners buzz whenever corporate scouts are spotted about town checking out the plant.\par \par \uc2 "We did everything right," said St. Clair Mayor Bill Cedar, who was part of an effort to woo Magna with tax breaks and the construction of a new water tower to supply the facility. He worries about how long it will take to replace the GM business and what the lack of work means for the city's bottom line. "Maybe the government should be slower to espouse programs that it thinks will be the new wave," he said.\par \par \uc2 At the start of the decade, EVs occupied a small but fast-growing corner of the U.S. auto market. Just 2% of vehicles sold in the U.S. in 2020 were EVs, nearly 80% of them made by Tesla. The Biden administration gave the EV market a big boost, offering $7,500 tax credits to buyers and implementing stricter federal air-quality mandates.\par \par \uc2 Between 2022 and 2025, the U.S. auto industry invested some $200 billion into new EV and battery factories. Suppliers raced to get a piece of the business.\par \par \uc2 Magna was all in. The 70-year-old parts maker based in Aurora, Ontario, has 156,000 employees in dozens of countries producing everything from seat frames to vehicle software.\par \par \uc2 Magna executives were counting on entirely new EV parts, such as the battery containers the company would make for GM, and the fact that components for EVs are often pricier than those for gas-powered vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Kotagiri told investors at the time that EVs were projected to deliver $4.5 billion in sales within a few years, a significant boost for a company that notched $42 billion in revenue in 2025. EV growth globally and in the U.S., he said, was inevitable, but would take place over the long term, not in a single burst.\par \par \uc2 At the time, the EV market in the U.S. was dominated by Tesla's sporty cars and smaller models such as Nissan's compact Leaf. GM and Ford had a different idea: Instead of crowding the market with more small cars, they would launch electric versions of their pickup trucks.\par \par \uc2 Magna won the job of building the metal box that would hold and protect the battery for GM's first electric truck: the Hummer EV. It put out word that it was building a factory and needed a site. Dozens of communities lobbied to be the place.\par \par \uc2 St. Clair was Magna's pick in part because of a $44 million incentive package offered by state, county and city governments. The town of 5,500 sits in a region of Michigan where the economy largely relies on manufacturing. Employment in the county surged during the tech boom of the 1990s, then sank amid the 2009 recession, when joblessness exceeded 25%. The unemployment rate now is 5%, and manufacturers in the area struggle to find enough workers.\par \par \uc2 GM, trying to catch up with Tesla, rolled out a $7 billion plan in 2022 to expand EV capacity and build up a supply chain. Big electric trucks were the centerpiece of GM's plan. The automaker said at the time it aimed to build 600,000 a year in North America by 2025.\par \par \uc2 A main section of the Magna factory opened in early 2022 and started churning out parts. The trucks garnered substantial buzz and preorders. GM announced it would follow the Hummer with an electric version of its Chevrolet Silverado pickup.\par \par \uc2 The sales boom never came. Last year, GM sold just over 11,000 Silverado EVs.\par \par \uc2 "We had rows and rows of battery trays. We were just boxing them up and throwing them in warehouses," said Daniel Martin, a former production supervisor at the factory. "I kept saying, 'What will happen if this falls through?' "\par \par \uc2 Martin watched as workers were laid off in waves, sometimes rehired, then laid off again. Eventually his job was cut. The plant, which was supposed to employ 900 workers, is down to a few hundred.\par \par \uc2 By early 2025, GM executives were scrambling to get a read on the U.S. market. President Trump's trade fights threatened to upend production plans. "It was like driving 24 Hours of Daytona in a fog. We had no idea where we were going," said GM CFO Jacobson, referencing the sports car endurance race while speaking at an employee town hall in January.\par \par \uc2 GM last year managed to avoid some of the worst-case tariff scenarios. EV sales, though, were about to fall off a cliff. Congress and the Trump administration did away with the $7,500 tax credit for buyers and eliminated significant fuel-economy and clear-air regulations. Without those sales boosters, GM needed to cut back capacity, Jacobson said.\par \par \uc2 GM's supplier contracts were struck with the expectation that GM would be building one million EVs a year. By December, the company was selling around 8,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 "The landscape shifted so dramatically that we had to reset, otherwise we were going to be climbing this mountain for more than a decade," Jacobson said at the town hall. Suppliers wanted stability -- and compensation. "Everybody was saying, 'You need to true me up,'" he said.\par \par \uc2 GM has paid some $400 million to suppliers whose contracts were either modified or eliminated. But those payoffs only partially cover the financial hit, and don't cover factory downtime while suppliers seek new business and retool plants.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of the manufacturers are banking on profits from selling parts five years out," said Bryan Powrozek, a partner in the manufacturing practice of accounting firm Wipfli. "What happens when all of a sudden they have big gaps in their production?"\par \par \uc2 Among the potential uncertainties: a Democratic administration and Congress might take over in a few years and revive EV-friendly policies. If automakers like GM were under the gun to go electric again, it would likely be a costly reversal for Magna and other suppliers.\par \par \uc2 Kotagiri said Magna has taken steps to make its manufacturing system more flexible, and to ensure the company has a diverse mix of products in the works. Some 80% of the parts Magna produces can be used in vehicles with any type of powertrain, he said.\par \par \uc2 "None of us has a crystal ball," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260402em420001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
