\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Irenic Discloses 2.5% Snap Stake --- Activist investor argues market capital should be closer to $35 billion\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elias Schisgall \par \uc2 406 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Activist investor \uc2 Irenic Capital Management\uc2  has built a roughly 2.5% stake in Snap and is asking the company's leadership to make a series of changes to boost the company's valuation, including laying off employees and closing or spinning off its Specs business.\par \par \uc2 Irenic sent a letter on Tuesday to Snap Chief Executive Officer \uc2 Evan Spiegel\uc2  arguing that the social-media company's market capitalization should be closer to $35 billion, as opposed to its current enterprise value of around $7.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 The activist investor said Snap has underperformed the Nasdaq by 444 percentage points since its initial public offering, adding that a dollar invested in Snap at the time of the IPO would be valued at 23 cents today.\par \par \uc2 "We are taking the unusual step of writing you a public letter because Snap isn't a typical public company," Irenic said. "Shareholders like us do not vote for Snap directors and we can't tell you what to do," Irenic added.\par \par \uc2 "We can only attempt to persuade you to do the right things for your shareholder-partners," the activist investor said in its letter.\par \par \uc2 Shares of Snap jumped 14% on Tuesday. The stock has fallen 47% over the past year.\par \par \uc2 In the letter, Irenic said Snap should rationalize its cost structure and the company has overhired. The activist investor cited examples of layoffs at Meta and Block designed to boost efficiency.\par \par \uc2 Irenic also recommended Snap get out of the augmented-reality-glasses business by shutting down or divesting itself of Specs. Irenic said Snap has spent more than $3.5 billion on Specs and is going through $500 million in cash annually.\par \par \uc2 Snap said it would spin off Specs into a distinct, wholly owned subsidiary in January, but Irenic suggested that it lacked confidence in the business even as a subsidiary. "At this point, if Specs cannot be funded on its own, it is time to shut it down," Irenic said.\par \par \uc2 Irenic also said Snap should revamp its approach to stock-based compensation, invest further in artificial-intelligence to improve advertising monetization, capitalize on the company's discounted valuation to buy back shares and give public shareholders one vote per share.\par \par \uc2 Snap Chair \uc2 Michael Lynton\uc2  said the company welcomes shareholder input and regularly discusses strategy, capital allocation and governance with shareholders.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260401em410001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
