\par\par \b \uc2 Oracle\uc2  To Slash Staffing Over Costs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera and Joseph De Avila \par \uc2 443 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Oracle\uc2  began to significantly reduce its workforce Tuesday while it continues to build out costly data centers for artificial-intelligence development.\par \par \uc2 The cloud-computing and database company is cutting jobs across its business lines, according to employees and several posts on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 . On social media, workers who said they were based in the U.S. and India posted about their positions being eliminated.\par \par \uc2 Some affected employees told The Wall Street Journal that they had received an early morning email from "Oracle Leadership" that thanked them for their work and informed them it was their last day.\par \par \uc2 "After careful consideration of \uc2 Oracle\uc2 's current business needs, we have made the decision to eliminate your role," an email to one affected employee says.\par \par \uc2 The full scope of the layoffs isn't yet clear, but some employees said internal metrics show the count of reductions thus far in the thousands. Analysts at investment bank TD Cowen earlier this year predicted \uc2 Oracle\uc2  would shed as many as 30,000 workers and sell some of its assets as it finances its AI infrastructure projects.\par \par \uc2 Oracle\uc2  employed about 162,000 people globally as of the end of May.\par \par \uc2 Representatives for \uc2 Oracle\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Oracle\uc2  shares rose 6% on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Shares in the company are down about 50% in the past six months as investors have expressed concern about the feasibility of its data-center-financing plan. The company raised its sales outlook in its most recent earnings report, while saying demand for AI continues to outpace supply.\par \par \uc2 Oracle\uc2  recently outlined in a regulatory filing that it would spend $500 million more on restructuring costs in the current fiscal year than previously reported, suggesting an acceleration in its job-cutting program.\par \par \uc2 Oracle\uc2 's project with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , known as Stargate, calls for several data centers in the next few years. Investors and analysts also consider the deal one of the riskiest because \uc2 Oracle\uc2  is taking on tens of billions of dollars in debt to partner with the unprofitable \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stargate's flagship site in Abilene, Texas, is partially operational, but \uc2 Oracle\uc2  won't immediately see much of the revenue from the overall project. Many investors and tech analysts are watching the company closely as a barometer for the AI boom-or-bust debate due to the difficulty of the Stargate project.\par \par \uc2 A number of other technology companies have laid off swaths of workers in recent months, in many cases working to automate more tasks with AI. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  laid off roughly 30,000 employees in six months. Block in February announced it was cutting nearly half of its staff.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260401em4100024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
