\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 How Top Firms Foster Careers of Employees --- High-performing companies hire out of college and teach soft skills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Theo Francis \par \uc2 754 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The 22 companies that got high marks across the board in a new analysis identifying the workplaces that provide the best earnings potential and professional growth share several practices, from hiring people early in their careers to training them relentlessly and aiding their advancement.\par \par \uc2 The Where You Work Matters List, created by the Burning Glass Institute and the Schultz Family Foundation, seeks to evaluate how good companies are for early-career opportunities, career growth and job stability.\par \par \uc2 It measures outcomes -- promotions, tenure, pay patterns and more -- and draws on the experiences of 12 million employees working in more than 500 different occupations at 1,700 companies.\par \par \uc2 Here's what top-ranked companies said they do to foster the careers of their employees:\par \par \uc2 Hire early on\par \par \uc2 Every year \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  hires most of its employees right after they graduate from college.\par \par \uc2 That way, the consumer-products giant can train them in its culture and processes and maintain a pipeline of internal talent. Its employees, in turn, get coaching from veteran colleagues and exposure to various jobs.\par \par \uc2 "We provide all the supporting systems to develop them, coach them and then give them different assignments," said Annie Huang, senior vice president for human resources for \uc2 P&G\uc2  in North America. On average, employees hold four roles in their first decade at the company.\par \par \uc2 Qualtrics\uc2 , which makes software to conduct surveys and track customer and employee experiences, likes the energy that early-career hires bring, including in customer service. "It's bringing up engagement," said Julia Anas, the company's global chief people officer.\par \par \uc2 Different experiences\par \par \uc2 Qualtrics\uc2  says it doesn't want its new hires to get stuck in the same job. It wants them to learn different functions across various parts of the company to build their experience.\par \par \uc2 "Growth and development doesn't just mean a promotion -- you also can expand and grow and develop laterally, and maybe in a different function area," Anas said.\par \par \uc2 To support the cross-pollination, \uc2 Qualtrics\uc2  has removed internal obstacles.\par \par \uc2 For example, managers don't have to approve transfers, Anas said. Last year, internal candidates filled 36% of the company's openings.\par \par \uc2 Teach the soft skills\par \par \uc2 Jenna Paukstis started at defense contractor \uc2 Northrop Grumman\uc2  23 years ago as an industrial engineer and went on to roles managing production of jet fighters and bombers.\par \par \uc2 Now she is in the process of taking over as vice president and general manager of the company's weapons-systems division.\par \par \uc2 Along the way, Paukstis benefited from formal leadership-training programs that gave her access to top leadership and peer coaching. It also included training in intangible skills, she said: "How to walk into a room, how to own a room, how you're projecting your posture and your voice."\par \par \uc2 Northrop Grumman\uc2  filmed some of the exercises, and critiqued Paukstis' performance.\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, managers also got coaching on how to effectively connect with employees on video calls.\par \par \uc2 Coach up for new roles\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  likes to expose early-career employees to a range of experiences. It rotates college graduates through a range of jobs within engineering, finance or human resources.\par \par \uc2 Training and education are key to developing the workers as they move through different positions and take on assignments requiring more advanced skills and responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 The automaker's General Motors University program, for example, teaches skills like leadership and communication alongside more technical skills. Likewise, employees who want to move into a skilled-trade apprenticeship in manufacturing or engineering can go through an internal certification program.\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 's first job as a co-op student was inspecting Pontiac hoods and fenders. Later, a company fellowship supported her getting an M.B.A.\par \par \uc2 Set schedules\par \par \uc2 High-performing companies don't leave worker development up to chance. They formalize discussions around career planning to make sure employees are getting support, feedback and guidance.\par \par \uc2 P&G\uc2 , for example, spaces goal-setting, career planning and performance reviews across the calendar.\par \par \uc2 Each summer, managers and their reports complete plans for the coming year, setting clear goals for results they intend to deliver. Early in the year, employees talk with managers about their career trajectory, which leads into more traditional performance reviews later in the year. That, in turn, feeds into decisions about raises.\par \par \uc2 The process reflects on employees -- and their bosses. "We're holding every manager accountable for developing their people," said \uc2 P&G\uc2 's Huang.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260401em410000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
