\par\par \b \uc2 GM to Boost Heavy-Duty Truck Output --- Automaker plans to run Michigan plant six days a week starting in June\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Otts \par \uc2 642 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  will run its heavy-duty truck plant in Michigan six days a week starting in June, citing strong demand for its most powerful pickups even as gas and diesel prices rise.\par \par \uc2 Flint assembly plant currently runs around the clock on three shifts, five days a week. The additional production day will allow the plant to churn out more Chevrolet Silverado and GMC Sierra pickups. The plant produces the heavy-duty versions of those trucks, the 2500 and 3500, with about 1,100 a day rolling off the line, according to a \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  official.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 General Motors\uc2  is making strategic adjustments to Flint Assembly's production schedule to align with strong customer demand," a GM spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 GM is increasing production at a time when U.S. gas prices have risen by more than 30% since the start of the Iran conflict in late February. Forecasters say U.S. new-vehicle sales will fall about 6.5% in the first quarter from a year earlier. GM's stock is down about 10% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In a note Monday, analysts at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said GM's move will add 40,000 to 50,000 trucks annually to the plant's current output of about 260,000. They added that it appears to be aimed at taking market share from Ford.\par \par \uc2 Ford's F-Series pickup truck is the top-selling vehicle in the U.S. and the automaker's biggest source of profit. The company has been struggling with production after its aluminum supply was disrupted last year by a fire at its supplier.\par \par \uc2 A GM spokesman declined to comment on competitive issues.\par \par \uc2 Ford says it is on track to increase its output of full-size trucks this year. In a statement, the company said it boosted the speed of F-Series Super Duty truck production at its factory in Kentucky and added a shift for F-150 trucks at a plant in Michigan. This summer, the four plants that build Ford's full-size trucks will skip their annual vacation shutdown weeks, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Heavy-duty pickups sold to individuals and businesses like construction firms are among the most profitable vehicles that Detroit makes. They are priced to start in the $50,000 range and, with options, can push up to six figures.\par \par \uc2 U.S. buyers continue to gobble up large pickups. The segment grew 3.7% in 2025 to about 2.4 million vehicles, while the overall industry grew about 2%, according to Motor Intelligence. Nearly three-quarters of large pickups sold in the U.S. last year were made by Ford or GM.\par \par \uc2 GM also builds the heavy-duty Silverado at a plant in Ontario, Canada. Since auto tariffs were introduced a year ago, GM has been maximizing production at U.S. factories to avoid the additional duties. The additional volume at Flint won't affect production at the Canadian plant, a GM spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Started in 1947, Flint Assembly is GM's longest-running assembly site in North America. It employs about 4,200 hourly workers, who will be mandated into overtime hours to cover the additional day of production.\par \par \uc2 Despite the rise in gas prices, dealers and auto executives have said they have noticed little change in consumer demand because of fuel concerns. Earlier this month, GM finance chief, \uc2 Paul Jacobson\uc2 , said the automaker has more demand than supply of bigger vehicles like its Cadillac Escalade and some full-size trucks.\par \par \uc2 "If you look at the historical models, usually it takes four to six months of sustained, high oil prices before people start to think, 'Well, maybe I should go for less mileage or maybe I should buy down,'" Jacobson said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260331em3v00012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
