\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Why Atlanta's TSA Woes Were Pronounced --- Hartsfield-Jackson workers were among hardest hit by federal impasse\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amira McKee, Chao Deng and Harriet Torry \par \uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Two numbers are rocking \uc2 Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport\uc2 : four and zero.\par \par \uc2 Four hours is how early airport officials suggested passengers arrive to make it through backed-up security lines. Zero is what thousands of \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  employees were due to receive from Friday paychecks, for the second time this year.\par \par \uc2 The world's busiest airport, Atlanta has become a chokepoint in the federal funding standoff in Congress. Security-screening lines stretched out to terminal sidewalks on several days this past week, frustrating passengers, airport managers and airline executives.\par \par \uc2 Officials in Atlanta and other airports said travelers could still face lengthy wait times in coming days. "You shouldn't expect lines to decrease over the weekend," said Ricky Smith, general manager of Hartsfield-Jackson.\par \par \uc2 A congressional impasse over funding the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  cut off paychecks for TSA agents, prompting hundreds of workers to quit and thousands more to call out. A Senate-approved measure to provide funding for DHS garnered resistance Friday from some House lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 President Trump instructed DHS to start the processing of paying TSA workers in an executive action on Friday. TSA officers should begin seeing paychecks as early as Monday, the agency said.\par \par \uc2 Since the partial government shutdown began Feb. 14, TSA officers have increasingly called out to work second jobs or save money on commuting. Hartsfield-Jackson has among the highest call-out rates in the U.S., with 41% of the TSA staff out Wednesday, the DHS said. The national average was around 11%.\par \par \uc2 If staffing continues to slide, Smith said, the airport could struggle to process passengers and could effectively shut down. Each day, Hartsfield-Jackson sees an average of 2,100 arrivals and departures and hosts 286,000 passengers.\par \par \uc2 The funding impasse marks the second time that a government shutdown delayed TSA workers' pay since last fall. This time, staffing levels deteriorated more rapidly because workers' bank accounts were depleted during the last shutdown and they have less savings to rely on, Smith said.\par \par \uc2 Salaries vary by location, but the mean annual wage for a TSA worker was $61,800 in 2024, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Officials from unions representing TSA workers said in recent weeks that workers have sold plasma, delivered groceries and signed up to drive for ride-hailing services to make ends meet. Younger workers are facing particular hardship, officials said, especially those who are paying student loans.\par \par \uc2 "Not only do you have your regular bills, but the loans that you took out during the last shutdown are also due," said Aaron Barker, the local \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2  union president covering Georgia airports. "Now we're nearly in April again, and here we are."\par \par \uc2 Barker attributed the high rates of call-outs to the region's cost of living. Many workers have hourlong commutes to Hartsfield-Jackson, he said, because their salaries make it difficult to live nearby.\par \par \uc2 Rising gas prices since the war in Iran have put added strain on their budgets, he said. That's been particularly hard in places like Los Angeles, where gasoline prices have topped $6 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 "Our folks are hanging on by a thread if even that," said Bobby Orozco Jr., a regional \uc2 AFGE\uc2  representative for Arizona, California and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Airports and communities that surround them have tried to cover gaps. Hartsfield-Jackson offers TSA officers two meal vouchers per shift and pays for their parking.\par \par \uc2 Houston Food Bank\uc2 , which distributes food and other essentials in southeast Texas counties, has been doing special distributions for TSA workers during the partial government shutdown, according to its president and CEO Brian Greene.\par \par \uc2 For travelers through Atlanta, the lines are hard to predict, swinging from multiple hours at peak times to just minutes during slower spells.\par \par \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2 , whose largest operations hub is Hartsfield-Jackson, said it is allowing customers to adjust their flights while in security lines without speaking to an agent.\par \par \uc2 Delta also waived its change fees for travelers through Atlanta, and other airlines including United, American and Southwest have rebooked guests stuck in security lines free of charge.\par \par \uc2 Some airports participate in the TSA's Screening Partnership Program, which contracts security-screening services to private companies. All federally staffed airports are eligible for the program, but most of the country's major airports don't participate.\par \par \uc2 Airlines have also tried to tell passengers that the security lines aren't bad everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Delta said Thursday that hubs serving regions including Boston, Detroit and Los Angeles have seen "reasonable" TSA wait times this past week.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260328em3s0002f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
