\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Finding the Best Place to Work --- An analysis looks at best employers for earnings potential and professional growth\b0\par\par\uc2 By Theo Francis and Andrew Mollica \par \uc2 919 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It hasn't been easy to find an employer that's a good fit.\par \par \uc2 Job seekers often have little more to guide them than word-of-mouth, social-media chatter and websites offering glimpses of pay and benefits. Many end up churning through jobs or finding themselves stuck in a career cul-de-sac.\par \par \uc2 Now, a new analysis provides a potential road map. It points to companies best at hiring early-career candidates, moving employees up through the ranks and providing a stable home base for a career -- both overall, and for specific roles.\par \par \uc2 The Where You Work Matters List, by the Burning Glass Institute and Schultz Family Foundation, looked at the job titles and career moves of more than 12 million people working at more than 1,700 U.S. companies and nonprofits from 2019 to 2024.\par \par \uc2 "My hope is that this can be a nutrition label for workers -- that it can give them more agency for making better-informed decisions that are of tremendous consequence to them," said Matt Sigelman, president of the Burning Glass Institute, a labor-market think tank focused on the future of work.\par \par \uc2 Just 22 companies, from \uc2 General Motors\uc2  to the \uc2 Mayo Clinic\uc2  and \uc2 Fidelity Investments\uc2 , received top marks across the board. Many have developed specific strategies to foster employees' careers.\par \par \uc2 Finding the right fit is critical in a job market headlined by layoffs and hiring freezes and clouded by artificial intelligence and automation. The kind of job insecurity that once seemed the province of hourly, blue-collar jobs haunts white-collar professions these days.\par \par \uc2 Especially now, workers are better off at companies with track records of advancing their employees' careers, rather than those treating workers as expendable, said \uc2 Rajiv Chandrasekaran\uc2 , a managing director at the Schultz Family Foundation. (The foundation, started by former \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Chairman and Chief Executive \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  and his wife, Sheri, focuses on issues of economic mobility.)\par \par \uc2 "Having mobility strength is like having done the physical training and having the whole canteen of water as you hike up that mountain," Chandrasekaran said.\par \par \uc2 The analysis tracked people working in 55,000 jobs and assessed their early-career opportunities, career growth and job stability -- including pay -- across 553 occupation groups. It crunched data from \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , Indeed, \uc2 Glassdoor\uc2  and dozens of other sources.\par \par \uc2 The top 20% of companies for each group were awarded a platinum designation, while the next 20% got a gold status. Companies with the most occupation-level distinctions could receive companywide platinum or gold designations.\par \par \uc2 The best place to work may not be obvious. Financial and investment analysts, for example, might find better jobs outside the financial industry. More tech companies than financial firms got top marks across the board for the analyst positions.\par \par \uc2 Other companies scoring well for financial-analyst careers: \uc2 Bath & Body Works\uc2 , \uc2 Nike\uc2  and \uc2 Verizon Communications\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Likewise, tech companies like \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  made up less than a quarter of the list's top-rated employers of data scientists. Just as many of the high-performing companies were in finance and insurance, including Capital One and \uc2 Citadel Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And most of the top-scoring companies for data scientists came from other industries entirely, including \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and \uc2 Domino's Pizza\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those working in food and beverage service should consider applying to top performers including \uc2 Costco\uc2 , Texas-based grocer H-E-B or even the restaurants inside \uc2 Ikea\uc2 's stores, according to the list.\par \par \uc2 Working at a top-scoring company has concrete benefits, the list's creators say. Employees at companies receiving top ranks overall are more likely to earn more, stay at least three years, be promoted within five years and land a better job elsewhere once they leave. The exact degree of benefit varies by occupation.\par \par \uc2 Chipotle Mexican Grill\uc2  received gold overall and platinum for early-career jobs. About half its occupation groups, including cooks and food-service managers, received platinum for both early-career and career growth. More received either platinum or gold in at least one area.\par \par \uc2 The company credits, in part, a clear promotion structure that can bring a restaurant line worker through six roles over 3 l/2 years to a step beyond restaurant manager, where annual cash and equity generally exceeds $100,000.\par \par \uc2 Education programs, including tuition reimbursement available within about four months of hire, help attract job candidates, and those that use the programs tend to stick around longer and get promoted faster, the company says.\par \par \uc2 Job candidates shouldn't rule out a company just because it didn't get high marks overall. These employers can still score well for particular occupations. Likewise, specific jobs at platinum companies may not score well.\par \par \uc2 "We're used to thinking about there being good companies and bad companies," Sigelman said. "The reality is that there are good jobs and bad jobs at any given company."\par \par \uc2 Newell Brands, maker of Rubbermaid containers and Yankee Candles, isn't in the top-ranked companies overall, but three-quarters of its job categories got high marks for career growth.\par \par \uc2 The company says it seeks to identify promising employees for promotion, encourages career planning alongside performance reviews and pores over internal data to spot potential matches for open positions, even among employees who haven't applied.\par \par \uc2 "We used to have a post-and-pray model," said Bill Huffaker, Newell's senior vice president for talent development. Now, "we go after passive candidates."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260326em3q0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
