\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  Taps New Leader for Work AI --- The tech giant wants its staff to be more nimble by adopting artificial intelligence\b0\par\par\uc2 By Meghan Bobrowsky \par \uc2 437 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  ' chief technology officer, \uc2 Andrew Bosworth\uc2  , is taking over supervision of the company's efforts to adopt artificial intelligence throughout its workforce, according to an internal memo that was viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 He will oversee \uc2 Meta\uc2  's "AI For Work" initiative, which was previously led by a different executive, Guy Rosen, the memo said, as the company seeks to be more nimble to remain competitive with AI-native startups with smaller staffs.\par \par \uc2 "As I've been digging in I've found we have a lot to be proud of. The early pilots, the willingness to pressure-test new ideas, and the speed at which we've enabled teams to embrace AI tools has created real momentum and sets us up for this next phase," Bosworth said in the note to employees.\par \par \uc2 Bosworth, who goes by Boz, has been with the company since 2006 and became CTO in 2022. His duties have included oversight of its metaverse operations, encompassing virtual and augmented reality.\par \par \uc2 In January, \uc2 Meta\uc2  cut 1,500 roles in its metaverse division after the technology hadn't gained the traction the company had anticipated, the Journal previously reported, and shifted some spending to other bets such as its AI glasses.\par \par \uc2 The company is now focused on driving AI adoption across its 78,000-person workforce to accelerate the pace of work, eliminate layers from its organizational structure and change the day-to-day jobs of its employees.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  's chief executive, \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  , has begun developing an AI agent to help him perform his duties, the Journal reported earlier this week. Employees across the company are being encouraged to create and use new AI tools, including a personal agent tool called MyClaw that has access to their chat logs and work files and can talk to colleagues on their behalf.\par \par \uc2 Bosworth is also overseeing another new organization at \uc2 Meta\uc2  focused on applying AI and supporting the teams building its new large language models. That organization is aiming to have teams of up to 50 individual contributors per one manager and be "AI native from day one," according to the internal memo shared with staff.\par \par \uc2 In the memo Tuesday, Bosworth said the company needs to align its internal technology stack to make sure it coheres over time. "That means looking at agent infrastructure, orchestration, and product experiences so everyone can build what they need but under a common, unified architecture that accrues to the benefit of all," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260325em3p0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
