\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 On the Clock: Hoping for a Big Raise? Check Your Expectations --- Tighter job market is having a knock-on effect on people's pay, with pay bumps elusive\b0\par\par\uc2 By Callum Borchers \par \uc2 934 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ann Schliemann made six figures as a project manager a couple of years ago. Her next job paid $90,000. Now she is interviewing for $80,000 positions after being laid off in December.\par \par \uc2 The corporate ladder feels more like a slide to people whose roles are being devalued in today's cooler job market.\par \par \uc2 While some Americans are still upping their earnings, federal data show overall wage growth has been slowing since the second half of 2022. More than a quarter of people who recently switched jobs took pay cuts, and 16.3% settled for lateral moves, according to \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With more applicants for fewer job openings, many of us are being forced to adjust our expectations around pay.\par \par \uc2 A compensation reset was probably inevitable after companies hoarded talent and dangled big raises a few years ago. And let's acknowledge that getting a business's size and pay scale just right isn't easy. Still, the job-market correction is sharper than a lot of workers and economists expected amid breathless hype about artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 It's debatable whether AI efficiencies are as responsible for layoffs and salary deflation as some executives claim. Automation can be a convenient cover to reverse postpandemic overhiring -- not to mention an attempt to signal that a business is on the cutting edge.\par \par \uc2 C-suite rationales, real or exaggerated, make little difference to those most affected. The bottom line is the U.S. economy shed 92,000 jobs in February, following months of slower hiring.\par \par \uc2 People like Schliemann enjoyed short-lived pay boosts, only to be jettisoned into an economy where everything costs more. Higher wages in recent years contributed to inflation, but the relationship doesn't work in reverse.\par \par \uc2 Schliemann is resigned to the likelihood of a pay cut, four months into her job search. At 64, she owns her Florida home outright but doesn't have enough savings to retire. In a relatively short time, she has seen prospective employers shift from competing for talent to sensing candidates' desperation.\par \par \uc2 "Companies don't have to offer as much as they used to," she says. "I'm to the point where I'll take whatever I can get."\par \par \uc2 Job seekers aren't the only ones feeling downward pressure on salaries. People who are employed can't assume the customary pay bump is coming this year.\par \par \uc2 "There's no automatic increase," says Ron Seifert, a senior client partner at consulting firm \uc2 Korn Ferry\uc2 . "Now it's about directing increases toward those that are differentiated in their performance, their potential or their skills."\par \par \uc2 This is a change from companies' practice of spreading "peanut-butter raises" more or less evenly across the entire team, he adds. Client surveys indicate an average raise this year will be 3.2% to 3.6%, but there may be a wide range. Highly valued employees could receive 1.5-to-2 times that amount. Others might get little or nothing.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, bonuses are shrinking, and fewer employees are receiving them, according to payroll software provider ADP.\par \par \uc2 It's a commentary on how bosses view their charges at the moment. Managers are focused on retaining key players; just about everyone else is replaceable.\par \par \uc2 Notably, what makes someone critical isn't necessarily what we assume. Rank-and-file workers tend to think high achievement is the ticket to raises and job security, but business leaders aren't doling out money for past performance.\par \par \uc2 "It really depends on where an organization sees the most value being created in the future," says Tauseef Rahman, a partner at professional-services firm \uc2 Mercer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Rahman helps companies identify growth areas and figure out how many employees they need to accomplish goals. He says people would be wise to honestly assess whether they fit the vision.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't pay to be the star of a dying division.\par \par \uc2 Leaving for another company may make sense if that firm is investing in your line of work. Just don't expect the move to come with a windfall.\par \par \uc2 The job switchers' premium -- the additional money people earn by jumping ship instead of staying put -- has been dwindling for a while. Early this year it shrank to 1.9 percentage points, according to ADP, the smallest since 2020.\par \par \uc2 This should prompt us to think differently about job hopping. Sizable raises have traditionally been primary motivators; security may now be the main reason to make a change. Some workers use the term "lily padding" to describe career moves that are more about finding safe landing spots, or learning skills to become more employable, than about scoring bigger paychecks.\par \par \uc2 Job coach Colleen Paulson, founder of Ageless Careers, doesn't make compensation-related promises and has stopped estimating how long it will take clients to find new roles. She figures it's better, or at least more honest, to manage expectations than to get people's hopes up.\par \par \uc2 "I'm just open about it, and I think it makes people feel better that they're not the only ones who might have to take a pay cut," she says.\par \par \uc2 In the current climate, we could all learn to think more like people who work on commission. They are used to up-and-down incomes, unlike many of us who have grown accustomed to consistent ups. We might even appreciate the good years more if we don't expect them to last forever.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260323em3n0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
