\par\par \b \uc2 REVIEW --- The 30-Year Debate Over the Minimum Wage Is Not Settled --- A paper upended the idea that minimum-wage increases are a job killer. Economists have been at it ever since.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 857 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par C4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For decades, Econ 101 held that raising the minimum wages killed jobs. Then, in 1994, economists \uc2 David Card\uc2  and \uc2 Alan Krueger\uc2  published a paper saying the opposite. Economists have been fighting ever since; today, the debate has never felt more relevant.\par \par \uc2 Nineteen states raised their minimum wages in January, giving an estimated 8.3 million workers a raise. Three more follow later this year. More workers now live in states that pay $15 or more per hour than those at the $7.25 federal minimum, according to the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How it all affects the job market remains deeply unsettled. At the heart of the debate: Does raising the wage floor reduce employment by making labor more expensive?\par \par \uc2 Card and Krueger's paper got its start in 1992, when New Jersey decided to raise its minimum wage to $5.05, while neighboring Pennsylvania aimed to leave it at the $4.25 federal minimum. For the two Princeton professors, this was an opportunity for a natural experiment.\par \par \uc2 The fast-food industry was a leading employer of minimum-wage workers. By surveying restaurants on either side of the border before and after New Jersey's wage increase, they could gauge the effect. In all, they sampled 410 fast-food restaurants.\par \par \uc2 What they found upended decades of economic orthodoxy: After New Jersey's minimum wage rose, fast-food employment actually increased relative to levels seen in Pennsylvania. The paper published in 1994 stated plainly: "We find no indication that the rise in the minimum wage reduced employment."\par \par \uc2 For the Clinton administration, the paper lent intellectual ballast to its case to raise the federal minimum wage. It seized on the research.\par \par \uc2 The blowback was intense. The restaurant lobby hated the paper. So did a lot of prominent economists.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to The Wall Street Journal in 1996, Nobel Laureate Milton Friedman said that raising the minimum wage would reduce demand for workers, just like raising prices on "gasoline or wheat or milk" would cut into demand. Another Nobel laureate, James Buchanan, was less measured: "Fortunately, only a handful of economists are willing to throw over the teaching of two centuries; we have not yet become a bevy of camp-following whores," he wrote to the Journal.\par \par \uc2 Economists \uc2 David Neumark\uc2  and William Wascher, supplementing data gathered by a think tank with ties to the restaurant industry, found employment in New Jersey had actually fallen relative to Pennsylvania. So, Card and Kreuger hit back with a second study using more comprehensive data.\par \par \uc2 This kind of back and forth has gone on for years, with studies and counterstudies that employ different data sets and methodologies. And while fewer economists are stridently opposed to minimum wages than in the 1990s, the field remains divided on the question of whether they hurt employment.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, \uc2 David Card\uc2  was among the winners of the economics Nobel for his work on natural experiments, including the minimum wage paper. He believes that Krueger, who died by suicide in 2019, would have won it with him had he lived.\par \par \uc2 While on the surface much of the debate is an argument over statistics, it is also about economic theory. In economics, "monopsony," the opposite of monopoly, describes markets where buyers hold bargaining power. In the labor market, those buyers are employers, and the idea that they can hold more power goes back to Adam Smith, who in 1776 argued that employers could more easily work together to keep wages low than workers could organize to raise them. Smoke-filled rooms of colluding owners aren't required, though. Any market where switching jobs is costly and workers' options are limited can give employers more power in setting wages.\par \par \uc2 To understand how this works, imagine a company that pays low wages, said \uc2 University of Massachusetts\uc2  economist \uc2 Arindrajit Dube\uc2 . It saves money, but also has higher vacancies and turnover -- because who wants a job that doesn't pay well? When a minimum wage hike forces the company to raise its pay, the job becomes more attractive, which can actually increase employment.\par \par \uc2 Wellesley College economist Sari Pekkala Kerr notes that while there has been a lot of action in mostly higher-income states, the federal minimum wage, which was last raised 16 years ago, still acts as the floor in 20 states.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of places haven't seen increases in such a long time that who knows what would happen," said Kerr.\par \par \uc2 Dube was one of the authors of a 2019 paper that showed that higher minimum wages didn't dent employment, and believes that monopsony helps explain why. But that doesn't mean he thinks minimum wage increases come free. Companies must absorb bigger labor costs or pass them on to customers. At some point, a wage hike can hurt jobs. The hard part is figuring out how far is too far.\par \par \uc2 "Are we hitting the right point?" said Dube. "The Goldilocks zone, if you will."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260321em3l0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
