\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Cesar Chavez Abuse Claims Stun California Labor Groups\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Barrett \par \uc2 658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Groups with ties to Cesar Chavez, the Hispanic labor and civil-rights icon, are distancing themselves from the leader who died more than 30 years ago, after allegations he abused women and girls at the height of his prominence in the 1960s and '70s.\par \par \uc2 The United Farm Workers Union, co-founded by Chavez, said Tuesday that it wouldn't participate in Chavez Day celebrations this year in light of the "deeply troubling allegations" that Chavez "engaged in inappropriate sexual behavior with women and minors." Cesar Chavez Day is a paid holiday in California and some other states and a nonpaid commemorative federal holiday celebrated on March 31.\par \par \uc2 The Cesar Chavez Foundation, a nonprofit established to continue Chavez's legacy, also said it was "deeply shocked and saddened by what we are hearing." The foundation said it would work with the UFW to "support the people who may have been harmed by his actions, and ensure we are united and guided by our commitment to justice and community empowerment." Chavez's son Paul serves as board chairman.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported Wednesday that multiple women claimed Chavez abused them as young girls or women. The women said they kept silent for decades to avoid tarnishing Chavez's legacy. The Wall Street Journal hasn't independently verified the sexual-abuse allegations.\par \par \uc2 One of his accusers, \uc2 Dolores Huerta\uc2 , was another UFW co-founder and a prominent figure in the Hispanic labor movement.\par \par \uc2 In a statement Wednesday, she said: "I am nearly 96 years old, and for the last 60 years have kept a secret because I believed that exposing the truth would hurt the farmworker movement I have spent my entire life fighting for."\par \par \uc2 Huerta said she had two sexual encounters with Chavez in the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 She said she felt pressure the first time because he was her boss and someone she admired. The second time, she said in the statement, shared on social media, she was "forced, against my will, and in an environment where I felt trapped."\par \par \uc2 She said she had children after both encounters and that she hid the pregnancies and arranged for the children to be raised by other families.\par \par \uc2 "The farm-worker movement has always been bigger and far more important than any one individual," she said, adding: "I have kept this secret long enough. My silence ends here."\par \par \uc2 In a statement, members of the Chavez family said they were "shocked and saddened to learn" of the alleged abuse. "As a family steeped in the values of equity and justice, we honor the voices of those who feel unheard and who report sexual abuse," the statement said.\par \par \uc2 Born March 31, 1927, near Yuma, Ariz., Chavez became a notable figure in Mexican-American culture. He deployed nonviolent tactics -- including boycotts, marches and hunger strikes -- to win rights for farm workers.\par \par \uc2 He rose to prominence with the successful Delano grape strike of 1965 -- 1970.\par \par \uc2 In the early 1970s, he launched a nationwide boycott of iceberg lettuce. He was jailed for defying a court order to end it. The California Supreme Court later overturned the injunction against boycotts.\par \par \uc2 Well-known figures, including Robert F. Kennedy, supported his movement; President Bill Clinton awarded him the Presidential Medal of Freedom.\par \par \uc2 When Chavez died in 1993 at the age of 66, his funeral resembled a final march for his legions of followers, and attracted an estimated 35,000 people.\par \par \uc2 Hispanic leaders have expressed dismay at the allegations and several other organizations have canceled or altered planned events honoring Chavez.\par \par \uc2 In Tucson, the Arizona Cesar E. Chavez + Dolores Huerta Holiday Coalition announced it would rename a planned Saturday event to the Comunidad y Labor Unity Fair.\par \par \uc2 Some Hispanic leaders criticized Chavez over the years as he came to view undocumented migrants as a threat to worker pay -- launching the "Illegals Campaign" to raise awareness about the issue.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260319em3j0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
