\par\par \b \uc2 Finance Bros to Tech Bros: Lay Off My Bloomberg Terminal --- Wall Street pros worship the tool. So when AI evangelists declared it 'cooked,' it was war.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabelle Bousquette \par \uc2 845 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A battle of insults and threats has broken out between the tech world and Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 What's got everyone so worked up? The same thing that starts most fights: business software.\par \par \uc2 A series of social-media posts went viral in recent days with claims that AI has created a worthy -- and way cheaper -- alternative to the Bloomberg terminal, a computer system that is like oxygen to professional investors.\par \par \uc2 Now "\uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  is cooked," some posters argued as they heralded the arrival of a newly released AI tool from startup \uc2 Perplexity\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most people would be thrilled by the prospect of a tool that promises to do for $2,400 a year what they currently pay $30,000 a year to get. Not Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 Traders and investment research teams have a sacred bond with the Bloomberg terminal, which they use to track and analyze real-time and historical pricing for a variety of asset classes, execute and record trades, and chat with customers and peers through one of its most popular features, Instant Bloomberg.\par \par \uc2 Some met their spouses on its chat feature. Others say they spend more time with it than they do their spouse. At least one commissioned a wedding cake in the shape of it. It is a status symbol, rite of passage and phantom limb wrapped into one.\par \par \uc2 So it was perhaps no surprise that the \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  doomsayers were met with a spirited and decisive backlash from a devoted fan-base who would rather resort to physical violence than let go of their personal and professional security blanket. The finance bros who worship at the altar of \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  have declared war on the tech evangelists who have put all their faith in AI.\par \par \uc2 To suggest that the terminal is replaceable is "laughable," said Jason Lemire, who jumped into the conversation on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 . (Ironically or not, his post also included an AI-generated image of churchgoers praying to the Bloomberg terminal).\par \par \uc2 "It seems quite obvious to me that those propagating that post are either just looking for easy engagement and/or have never worked in a serious financial institution," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  defenders questioned the original posters' sanity and experience. Some went as far as suggesting they turn themselves into the police for their egregious assertions. Others invoked memes of Penn Jillette, the comedian known for his docuseries, "Bullshit!"\par \par \uc2 But the vibe coders wouldn't be swayed, accusing the critics of "pearl clutching" and ignorance over the vast promise of AI.\par \par \uc2 "There's a definite naivete," Tom Fry, who built trading software at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  before co-founding AI startup Agentcy, said about the finance community's response. "Everything is disruptible."\par \par \uc2 This has been an ongoing refrain from the tech community in recent weeks as vibe-coding tools in products like Claude Code and the newly released Perplexity Computer pledge to help companies re-create cheaper versions of popular software systems.\par \par \uc2 But when they came for Bloomberg, something was different. Many in the Bloomberg camp claimed the original posters were paid by \uc2 Perplexity\uc2  to orchestrate the campaign and weren't properly disclosing it.\par \par \uc2 Perplexity\uc2  has denied this, saying the conversation was 100% organic. The firm's goal was never to go head-to-head with \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 , said Chief Business Officer \uc2 Dmitry Shevelenko\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But he said he wasn't surprised to see the conversation there take off. \uc2 Perplexity\uc2  CEO Aravind Srinivas had written in 2024 about developing "the friendly UI and affordable \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  terminal for the masses," in an X post that drew its own fair amount of polarized responses.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  has beaten back many competitors since it launched in 1982 and in recent years has been embedding its own AI features.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Linton, the co-founder and CTO of AI startup Bold Metrics and an avid Perplexity Computer user, said it's rare for a single AI prompt to generate anything close to what \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  does. That said, he added that tools like this can lay "a really good foundation for a financial application. And that really has not been possible before."\par \par \uc2 Others aren't so sure.\par \par \uc2 Michael Terry, an institutional investment manager who used the terminal for more than 30 years, said he used a prompt circulating online to try to vibe code a \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  replica on \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's Claude.\par \par \uc2 "It was laughable at best, horrific at worst," he said.\par \par \uc2 Shevelenko acknowledged there are some aspects of the terminal that can't be replicated with vibe coding, including some of \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 's proprietary data inputs.\par \par \uc2 The live chat network, which includes 350,000 financial professionals, would also be hard to re-create.\par \par \uc2 "I love Bloomberg. And I know most people that use \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  are very, very loyal and extremely happy," said Lemire. His message to the techies? "There's nothing that you can vibe code in a weekend or even like over the course of a year that's going to come anywhere close."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260318em3i0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
