\par\par \b \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Retools Strategy on AI Products\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 510 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  is reorganizing the teams that work on the different versions of its flagship Copilot AI product, altering a strategy that some employees said created a disjointed user experience and consumer confusion.\par \par \uc2 The software giant is unifying the teams that work on its \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  365 Copilot productivity offerings and the consumer version of Copilot, according to a memo from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Satya Nadella\uc2  reviewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Andreou, who leads product and growth for \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  AI, will become executive vice president of Copilot, in charge of its design, product, growth and engineering. \uc2 Mustafa Suleyman\uc2 , Microsoft AI's chief executive, will now focus primarily on the company's proprietary AI models and its drive toward what it calls superintelligence. The \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  365 apps will be led by a group of executives that include \uc2 Ryan Roslansky\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  and an executive vice president at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With the changes, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  moves away from having distinct commercial and consumer Copilot products and toward one product experience across its business and consumer applications.\par \par \uc2 The new setup will enable \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  to deliver more coherent and competitive experiences that can evolve as AI models advance, Nadella said in his memo. He also said its AI models are more critical than ever to its success during the next decade.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's shake-up is happening as the company is working to drive up sign-ups for its commercial \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  365 Copilot while increasing adoption of its consumer version, which lives on its own app and on the Edge browser.\par \par \uc2 Both efforts have faced headwinds.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  in February reported that it had sold 15 million \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  365 Copilot "seats," a fraction of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  365's base of 450 million-plus paid seats. The company last year said it had over 150 million monthly active Copilot users across its first-party platforms. \uc2 Google\uc2 's Gemini has more than 650 million monthly users, while ChatGPT has about 900 million.\par \par \uc2 A big challenge for Copilot has been a lack of a cohesive experience across the different Copilots and the organizational silos between teams, the Journal reported in February, with customer surveys by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  showing that users have been confused by the multiple versions of Copilot.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  hired Suleyman in 2024 to lead its consumer Copilot and build AI models that could compete with those from \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and other leading labs. The hiring was seen at the time as a major step by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  to bulk up its AI suite. The effort to train proprietary models has been hampered by a shortage of computing capacity, the Journal also reported. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's models have lagged behind others in benchmark tests.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has tried to entice more users to its consumer Copilot by launching new features, such as a tool announced last week that lets the chatbot dispense personalized healthcare advice. In its most recent earnings report, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  said it was allocating more computing power to improve its Copilot products because it had gained confidence in its ability to monetize them.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260318em3i0001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
