\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: New York City's $30 Minimum-Wage Proposal Is Rattling Small Businesses\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng and Kate King \par \uc2 666 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A proposal to raise the minimum wage in New York City to $30 an hour is drawing cheers from working-class New Yorkers struggling to afford the city and sending chills through a business community confronting rising costs on other fronts.\par \par \uc2 A bill introduced this past week in the City Council would raise New York's minimum wage to the highest in the country -- for any city or state. If the measure passes, it would take the minimum hourly wage from $17 to $30 by 2030 for large businesses; businesses that employ fewer than 500 people would hit the $30 mark by 2032.\par \par \uc2 For small businesses especially, a wage increase would come on top of sky-high rent and surges in utility and insurance costs since the pandemic. "It's just going to get to the stage where a chef or a waitress or a bartender who has a dream of opening a restaurant -- it's just not possible," said Sean Hayden, who employs 200 people across his five restaurants and a cocktail lounge in Manhattan, and pays his tipped workers the city's $17 minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Many other cities and states have boosted minimum-wage pay in recent years, including Denver, San Jose, Calif., and West Hollywood, Calif. Seattle now has one of the country's highest minimum wages, at $21.30 an hour. In Los Angeles, the City Council last year approved raising the minimum hourly wage from $20.32 to $30 by 2028 for hotel and airport workers. Hotel owners said the move would hurt the industry.\par \par \uc2 How quickly the New York bill goes up for a vote will likely depend in part on how much political capital Mayor Zohran Mamdani puts behind it. Mamdani won the election on a campaign to make the city more affordable and at the time voiced support for $30 by 2030. A spokesman said the mayor's team is reviewing the bill.\par \par \uc2 The proposal could face legal challenges as the city's current minimum wage is set by the state, and New York state law doesn't explicitly give the city authority to set its own.\par \par \uc2 An hourly wage of $30 amounts to an annual salary of $62,400, still short of living wage standards by some measures. According to the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 's Family Budget Calculator, a single person needs $83,262 a year in the New York metro area to pay for decent shelter, food, transportation and other necessities, with about 35% going toward housing. A couple raising one child would require $142,229, with 23% going toward housing and 11% toward child care.\par \par \uc2 Many businesses pay well above $17-an-hour minimum wage to compete for employees.\par \par \uc2 Joelle Jean, a member of Amazon's employee's union who works nights at an Amazon warehouse on Staten Island, says she struggled to get by in New York even on her hourly wage, $26.15. She said she couldn't move out of her shelter in Brooklyn, in large part because she gets turned down by landlords scrutinizing her salary when she inquires about apartments. "Thirty dollars to me means getting my own place, to live with a bit of dignity."\par \par \uc2 An Amazon representative didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Seyedian owns a cleaning business that starts its maids at $27 an hour in New York City. He said a phased increase to $30 is fair given the cost burdens shouldered by low-income families.\par \par \uc2 Other business owners are concerned about making ends meet. "As much as I would like to pay $30, we don't have money," says Moe Chan, who is trying to expand his Burmese coffee-and-tea distribution business in Queens. He employs two part-time workers on minimum wage and says he is struggling with rent and the impact of President Trump's tariffs on imports.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260314em3e0001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
