\par\par \b \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  Donates $100 Million To Boost Training in Skilled Trades\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Pitcher \par \uc2 451 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , whose infrastructure funds finance everything from data centers and bridges to toll roads, is now making an investment in the workers those projects will require in the future.\par \par \uc2 The money manager is committing $100 million of grant capital to fund and expand training programs in the skilled trades, including plumbers and electricians. Its new Future Builders initiative will work with nonprofit and workforce-development partners in supporting workers at each stage of their development -- beginning before they serve apprenticeships and all the way through the point that they earn licenses in their chosen trade.\par \par \uc2 The donation, announced at \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 's infrastructure summit in Washington on Wednesday, comes while the world's largest asset manager is making a major push into private-infrastructure investing. Chief Executive \uc2 Larry Fink\uc2  has said that rising government deficits, combined with aging U.S. infrastructure, present a multitrillion-dollar investment opportunity that will need to be plugged by private investors in the coming decade.\par \par \uc2 While artificial-intelligence advancements are making some white-collar workers nervous, \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  researchers are projecting that demand for electricians, HVAC technicians, plumbers and ironworkers is going to outpace the current training capacity for new workers in skilled trades -- creating a potential growth bottleneck.\par \par \uc2 "Capital alone is not enough," Fink said. "People are central to building our nation's future."\par \par \uc2 Fink and \uc2 Adebayo Ogunlesi\uc2 , the billionaire chief executive of \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 's infrastructure investing arm, were in Washington on Wednesday to court lawmakers and tout the need to modernize old systems, build new energy and digital infrastructure, and train the workers required to do so.\par \par \uc2 Ogunlesi's Global Infrastructure Partners made a splash last year with a Trump-endorsed deal to acquire dozens of global ports, including two coveted terminals on either end of the Panama Canal from a Hong Kong-based company. That yet-to-close deal is in limbo after Beijing sought to intervene.\par \par \uc2 More recently, \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  and \uc2 GIP\uc2 -led groups struck a $20 billion deal to buy \uc2 Aligned Data Centers\uc2  and a $10.7 billion deal for power company AES, as \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  seeks to position itself as a key player in the AI infrastructure build-out.\par \par \uc2 Private-infrastructure investing on behalf of institutional clients who seek stable returns is a key growth area for \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , which grew to a $14 trillion behemoth primarily by leading on low-fee, public stock and bond index investing. The asset manager is now expanding into higher-fee and more hands-on private-asset businesses like owning and operating infrastructure and lending private capital to companies -- telling investors last year that it aims to raise $400 billion from clients for private funds by 2030.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260312em3c0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
