\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: After a Long Ride, Engine Of U.S. Labor Market Stalls\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harriet Torry, Danny Dougherty and Alana Pipe \par \uc2 508 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 This time, healthcare isn't here to save the labor market.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. unexpectedly shed 92,000 jobs last month, the second-largest monthly decline since the Covid-19 pandemic and a fresh warning that the labor market continues to struggle after limping through 2025.\par \par \uc2 Though unemployment remains relatively low, jobs are declining in most sectors, including strike-fueled losses in healthcare. Put together, January's healthcare-driven jobs surge now looks more like an anomaly than a turnaround.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at some of the key trends in the latest Labor Department report.\par \par \uc2 Broad weakness\par \par \uc2 February's losses were broad-based, hitting sectors that had shown some signs of improvement in early 2026, like professional and business services, construction and manufacturing jobs.\par \par \uc2 Leisure and hospitality -- often a bellwether for consumers' willingness to spend on services like vacations and restaurant meals -- cut jobs for the second month in a row, slashing 27,000. The labor market has now shed jobs in five out of the past nine months.\par \par \uc2 Healthcare U-turn\par \par \uc2 The healthcare and social assistance category -- which includes an array of jobs, including hospital workers and home-health aides -- has been the main engine of jobs growth over the past couple of years. But it cut nearly 19,000 jobs in February, marking the first decline in over four years and a sharp U-turn after adding 116,000 in January.\par \par \uc2 What happened? The Labor Department said strike actions were the driving force behind the 37,000 jobs lost in physicians' offices. Hospitals added jobs. Still, payrolls declined at diagnostic labs and at nursing and residential-care facilities.\par \par \uc2 White-collar struggle\par \par \uc2 White-collar workers are facing an increasingly unforgiving labor market. Companies are reluctant to hire due to uncertainty around tariffs, still-high borrowing costs and signs that artificial intelligence could replace college-educated workers' jobs. February's numbers suggest these trends are continuing.\par \par \uc2 Another rough month means the information sector, which includes telecommunications, data processing and IT workers, has shed more than 300,000 jobs since its recent peak in late 2022.\par \par \uc2 Professional and business services, which includes people working in the legal, accounting and consulting sectors, have cut about half a million jobs since mid-2023. Financial activities, which includes jobs in banking, insurance and real-estate leasing, posted the biggest month-over-month job gains in a year. Still, there are slightly fewer jobs in those fields than there were a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Race and ethnicity\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate held steady for white workers in February at 3.7%. But it rose for Asian, Hispanic and Black workers.\par \par \uc2 The latter group lost ground in February after some recent improvements, adding to a longer-running warning sign for the broader labor market. Economists watch for climbing Black unemployment because Black workers are more likely to hold low-skill or entry-level jobs, which are often cut first when the economy weakens.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich637\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal9555
