\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Jobs Data, Oil Surge Put Fed in Tight Spot\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Timiraos \par \uc2 613 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's biggest fear has always been having to choose between fighting inflation and protecting jobs. Friday's employment report brought that dilemma a step closer.\par \par \uc2 Just weeks ago, a solid January jobs report had raised hopes that the labor market was finding its footing after months of fitful hiring. Friday's report wilted those green shoots, showing a loss of 92,000 jobs in February and an increase in the unemployment rate to 4.4%. The readings ended optimism about stabilization and reanimated worries that the labor market has been quietly deteriorating.\par \par \uc2 For Washington, the timing could hardly be worse. Fed policymakers were just beginning to grapple with new threats to energy and commodity prices following the U.S.-Israel military campaign in Iran, which has closed key global shipping lanes and threatens to shut down swaths of energy production.\par \par \uc2 "Let's not overreact to one month's numbers, but an environment in which inflation is rising and the unemployment rate is also rising, that's not any fun for the central bank," \uc2 Chicago Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Austan Goolsbee\uc2  said Friday.\par \par \uc2 The report landed at an awkward moment for the White House, which has spent the past year arguing that its program of deregulation, trade deals and tax cuts would unleash a new era of job creation. Instead, the opposite appears to be happening: Hiring has slowed, and the administration's fluid tariff program and now the military campaign in Iran have injected the kind of uncertainty that businesses cite when they stop adding workers.\par \par \uc2 The Middle East conflict has raised the prospect of another bout of price increases in an economy where inflation has been above the Fed's 2% goal for five years. A jobs report that might otherwise have opened the door to rate cuts was instead wrapped in inflationary risk.\par \par \uc2 "You're going to see a spike in gasoline prices," Fed governor \uc2 Christopher Waller\uc2  said on \uc2 Bloomberg Television\uc2  on Friday morning, before the report was released. "That's what American citizens are going to see when they go to the pump, and they're going to be a little shocked."\par \par \uc2 A central bank that finds itself simultaneously watching the job market weaken and inflation risks re-emerge has few good options. The Fed can't easily cut rates to protect jobs without risking an acceleration in prices that are already high. It can't hold firm without risking a labor market that may be more fragile than it looks.\par \par \uc2 The Fed has often treated energy shocks as temporary disruptions it can look through, riding out rather than reacting to price swings. But the central bank has faced a series of supply shocks since 2020: the pandemic, Russia's invasion of Ukraine, the administration's tariff program, and now the war in Iran. That could limit the Fed's flexibility if it worries about another short-term rise in prices creating knock-on effects.\par \par \uc2 "This is now potentially an echo of Russia invading Ukraine," \uc2 Minneapolis Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2  said Monday. He referred to the Fed's misjudgment in 2021, when it dismissed a surge in inflation as likely to be temporary. "If this ends up being another commodity shock around the world, do we really want to do another transitory 2.0?"\par \par \uc2 Some analysts cautioned that the headline number in the payroll report was depressed by labor strikes in the health sector, suggesting the underlying picture may be somewhat less dire. But even stripping that out, hiring has been weak in recent months, underscoring that the softness is neither new nor narrow.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260307em3700027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
