\par\par \b \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  Lays Off 2,500 Employees\b0\par\par\uc2 By AnnaMaria Andriotis \par \uc2 348 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  is laying off around 3% of its workforce or about 2,500 people, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The cuts are affecting employees in the bank's three major divisions, the people said, which are investment banking and trading, wealth management and investment management. They are tied to shifting business and location priorities -- as well as individual job performance -- and are occurring both in the U.S. and abroad, one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 The moves come after the bank reported a banner year in 2025. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , which has around 83,000 employees, posted record annual revenue in its investment banking and trading division as well as in its wealth-management unit last year.\par \par \uc2 Many of the job cuts took place on Wednesday, though they have been occurring since last week, the people familiar with the matter said. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  has made several rounds of job cuts in recent years.\par \par \uc2 In the wealth-management division, this round of layoffs have included private bankers and back-office staff positions, the people said. Some of those impacted work on doling out mortgages to wealth-management clients, some of the people said.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and its Wall Street peers reported one of the strongest years on record in 2025, as big companies struck more deals, trading desks kept busy navigating market volatility and the wealthy continued to spend.\par \par \uc2 In the fourth quarter, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  reported a 13% jump in the wealth-management division's revenue, which typically accounts for close to half of the firm's revenue.\par \par \uc2 Big companies have laid off thousands of white-collar workers over the past year, in some cases pointing to increased efficiency created by artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Late last month, Jack Dorsey unveiled plans to slash 40% of the workforce -- or more than 4,000 employees -- at his company, Block, citing rapidly improving AI models as a primary reason. Some analysts are skeptical of that rationale and say he is capitalizing on a chance to slash costs at a company with excessive staffing.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260305em350001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
