\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  CEO Defends Pentagon Work to Staff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Berber Jin, Marcus Weisgerber and Amrith Ramkumar \par \uc2 854 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  CEO \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2  defended his decision to allow the Pentagon to use its tools for classified work, fielding questions from staff at an all-hands meeting Tuesday about the principles behind the decision.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  announced its \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  deal on Friday, hours after Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  designated rival \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  a supply-chain risk, a move rarely used against a U.S. company. After backlash against the potential for \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's deal with the Pentagon to allow for mass surveillance, the company changed its agreement to explicitly state domestic surveillance isn't allowed.\par \par \uc2 Altman said that while he didn't regret signing a deal with the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 , he wished he hadn't announced the decision so quickly, telling staff that it looked "opportunistic" and "not united with the field," according to remarks viewed by The Wall Street Journal. His remarks echoed a memo he shared with staff and on X Monday in which he said the deal "looked opportunistic and sloppy."\par \par \uc2 A groundswell of AI researchers at the company and across Silicon Valley spent the past few days criticizing Altman and the company for what they saw as capitulation to the Pentagon by essentially agreeing to a deal that allowed AI to be used in all lawful cases. Some publicly peppered the CEO with questions on X, while employees made their case on \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's \uc2 Slack\uc2  channel. \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's Claude overtook ChatGPT as the most popular app in \uc2 Apple\uc2 's app store for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Many \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  employees had called for the company to sign a deal that explicitly banned the use of its technology for mass surveillance and fully autonomous weapons. \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  previously rejected the agreement that \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  accepted and has been unusual in opposing some of the administration's policies.\par \par \uc2 Altman said in the all-hands meeting that it had been an unpleasant few days. "To try so hard to do the right thing and get so absolutely like, personally crushed for it -- and I know this is happening to all of you too, so I feel terrible for subjecting you all to this -- is really painful."\par \par \uc2 "I think this was an example of a complex but the right decision with extremely difficult brand consequences and very negative PR for us in the short term," Altman said.\par \par \uc2 News Corp\uc2 , owner of The Wall Street Journal, has a content-licensing partnership with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's Friday announcement blew open a long-simmering debate among AI researchers -- many of whom come from academic backgrounds -- about whether they can trust business leaders to responsibly deploy their technology. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  employees peppered Altman during the Tuesday meeting with questions about the boundaries of the agreement.\par \par \uc2 Altman said the government was willing to give \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  influence over how its technology was deployed, and that he wanted his company to have a seat at the table in shaping future decisions.\par \par \uc2 A strong U.S. military had been a "great benefit to all of humanity over the last 250 years," Altman said, adding that he respects that the military gets to make decisions. "Clearly, the military has done things that I extremely disagree with, and am sure will do more in the future," he said.\par \par \uc2 In the meeting, Altman said that \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  was also looking at a contract to deploy on all \uc2 NATO\uc2  classified networks. An \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  spokeswoman later said Altman misspoke and that the \uc2 NATO\uc2  opportunity was for unclassified networks. Representatives for \uc2 NATO\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  has largely embraced President Trump's domestic AI strategy. Since Trump took office, the company has announced large investments with the administration to build data centers across the country. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  President \uc2 Greg Brockman\uc2  and his wife donated $25 million to a Trump-aligned super political-action committee last year and are spending millions more to promote his AI agenda in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's weekslong feud with the Pentagon over the startup's effort to limit the agency's ability to use its technology for mass domestic surveillance and autonomous weapons created an industry firestorm over how AI is used in the military and who should call the shots.\par \par \uc2 After Claude played a role in the January military operation that captured Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro, an \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  employee asked a \uc2 Palantir\uc2  counterpart how Claude was used, the Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  officials found out and were upset, people familiar with the matter said. \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  has said it was a routine call between partners.\par \par \uc2 Emil Michael\uc2 , the undersecretary of defense for research and engineering, said Tuesday at an event hosted by venture firm \uc2 Andreessen Horowitz\uc2  in Washington that a "chill goes up your spine" when a company asks a question like that.\par \par \uc2 "The soul of their model, their constitution, which is not the U.S. Constitution, can't be dictating our command and control environment, telling generals and war fighters what to do and not do," Michael said. Strikes on Iran over the weekend were planned with the involvement of Anthropic's Claude models, the Journal reported.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260304em340001t\par }