\par\par \b \uc2 The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  Gets a Makeover\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Harned \par \uc2 842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  has long been biased in favor of unions over businesses at the expense of workers. An independent federal agency that oversees workplace policies governing tens of millions of employees, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  finally is ready to put workers first, thanks to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 The president has appointed a quorum of new board members, who are poised to deliver reform and results. Yet labor policy can't be fixed overnight. As I show in a new report, a combination of federal laws, federal court rulings and regulations have stacked the deck against workers, depriving them of the time and information they need to control their future.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  shows how broken federal labor policy is. It is unfairly partial to union interests. Due to unclear labor laws, it frequently flip-flops on policies in ways that hurt workers, most recently during the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Consider how the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has wrongly defined "threats" in ways that hurt workers' rights. The agency has a history of excusing union supporters' threats against workers. The Biden \uc2 NLRB\uc2  even brought back a standard that makes it difficult for employers to discipline union organizers for using harassing language, including racist and sexist language. Decades of legal precedent have created loopholes that can shield unions from liability for certain types of property damage during labor disputes. Unions can confront workers during breaks, before or after work, and even at home in front of their families. This can make workers feel pressured or threatened, and they have no legal way to stop unions from contacting them.\par \par \uc2 Businesses can't (and shouldn't) get away with such actions. So why are unions allowed to behave this way? The Biden \uc2 NLRB\uc2  further tilted the scales toward unions by limiting employers' ability to talk about unionization in required all-staff meetings, saying such conversations could be threatening. In reality, such meetings allow businesses to provide their side of the story, which employees deserve to hear before voting to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Under the National Labor Relations Act, businesses aren't allowed to promise workers pay raises, promotions, benefits or other things that might discourage them from supporting a union. But the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has said unions can promise workers anything, even if they have neither the intention nor the ability to deliver. Unions may also seed workplaces with secret organizers -- known as salts -- who monitor colleagues and promote unionization without anyone knowing their true identity. Workers deserve the truth, especially since the labor law requires businesses to disclose when they hire someone to talk to workers about union organizing.\par \par \uc2 Workers also deserve to know when a union is targeting them. The law allows unions to force an election when only 30% of workers sign cards supporting it. But that means 70% of workers can be kept in the dark until it's time to vote. The Biden \uc2 NLRB\uc2  allowed a union to be recognized based on "card check" results even after a majority of workers vote against unionizing if the union can convince the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  that a business acted unfairly. The Biden \uc2 NLRB\uc2  also approved "ambush elections," giving workers as little as a few weeks to learn the facts about unionization and preventing employers -- especially small ones -- from providing employees with the time needed for full information.\par \par \uc2 Nor does the mistreatment of workers end after a union is elected. They have no easy way to decertify a union that no longer meets their needs. The Biden \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has allowed so-called blocking charges, in which unions prevent decertification until all allegations of unfair labor practices have been litigated. That makes it possible for unions to file endless lawsuits, maintaining their power even when a clear majority of workers want out.\par \par \uc2 Unions are also largely exempt from federal antitrust laws, allowing them to bargain across companies without being treated like businesses conspiring to limit competition. While meant to protect workers, the resulting agreements ultimately can raise employer costs, discourage hiring, and lead to job losses in the long term -- classic consequences of anticompetitive behavior.\par \par \uc2 Workers have labored under these unjust policies for nearly a century. They deserve better. In the short run, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  can help American workers by reversing the Biden rulings that strengthen unions and restrain businesses at workers' expense. The board also could end the Biden backdoor card-check scheme, prevent unions from using harassing language, and free employers to talk to workers about unionization. But a future \uc2 NLRB\uc2  with members appointed by another president could reverse these policies. Workers ultimately need Congress to pass better labor laws that will last.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in Congress have introduced many bills that would ensure workers are treated with more respect. These bills deserve serious consideration before the midterm elections. Workers need short-term action from the NLRB and lasting reform through legislation. The Trump administration and Congress can work together to deliver workers a truly level playing field.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harned is president of Harned Strategies LLC and a senior fellow at the Institute for the American Worker.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260304em340000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
