\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- How Dorsey Explained Cuts at Block\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Dummett \par \uc2 519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jack Dorsey isn't wasting any time. The chairman and co-founder of Block is cutting 40% of the payments company's workforce, or more than 4,000 employees, betting artificial-intelligence tools can fill the gap.\par \par \uc2 Here's how he explained the decision.\par \par \uc2 This isn't about cutting costs\par \par \uc2 Dorsey said Block, which owns Square and Cash App, is financially sound. Instead, the tech entrepreneur argues that companies need flatter structures and fewer employees to thrive in the AI era.\par \par \uc2 "Intelligence tools have changed what it means to build and run a company," Dorsey said Thursday on a call with analysts. "A significantly smaller team using the tools we're building can do more and do it better."\par \par \uc2 Block is already seeing the change internally, he added, "and intelligence tool capabilities are compounding faster every single week."\par \par \uc2 Block's workforce had surged\par \par \uc2 Block's workforce surged during the Covid-19 pandemic as the company built out its Cash App business alongside Square.\par \par \uc2 The payments firm ended 2019 with 3,835 workers but employed 12,985 people by the end of 2023. The cuts announced Thursday will bring its workforce back to levels last seen during 2021.\par \par \uc2 "Yes we over-hired during covid," Dorsey wrote on X in response to a user highlighting the company's pandemic-era additions.\par \par \uc2 He attributed the head count growth to building two separate company structures, and adding complexity by pushing into new lines of work.\par \par \uc2 Better to move fast than slow\par \par \uc2 A big challenge for companies wanting to restructure is how to minimize disruption and maintain the morale of employees sticking around. In a memo to Block staff that was posted on X, Dorsey said he had two options: To cut gradually over time or "be honest about where we are and act on it now."\par \par \uc2 Although Block has had job cuts in previous years, Dorsey said he favors the second option.\par \par \uc2 "Repeated rounds of cuts are destructive to morale, to focus, and to the trust that customers and shareholders place in our ability to lead," Dorsey wrote.\par \par \uc2 A harbinger for U.S. Inc.?\par \par \uc2 Employees across companies should brace themselves for similar large-scale layoffs, Dorsey predicted.\par \par \uc2 "Within the next year, I believe the majority of the companies will reach the same conclusion and make similar structural changes," Dorsey told analysts. "I don't think we're early to this realization. I think most companies are late."\par \par \uc2 Changes for customers too\par \par \uc2 Large-scale layoffs at Block won't just impact the company's workers. Its customers will feel the shift too, Dorsey said.\par \par \uc2 The use of AI tools means customers will be able to build their own features directly, he said, on top of the company's capabilities.\par \par \uc2 A risky move\par \par \uc2 Dorsey acknowledged that such a big cull could risk Block's ability to serve its customers in the short term. But he remains defiant.\par \par \uc2 "A decision at this scale carries risk. But so does standing still," Dorsey said in his memo.\par \par \uc2 "I made this decision, and I'll own it," he wrote.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich309\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4635
