\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 The U.S. vs. Antisemitism at UCLA\b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The anti-Israel encampments on college campuses are gone, but accounting for the antisemitism they revealed is still a moral duty for universities. Some healthy reckoning has arrived in a Justice Department lawsuit that says the University of California failed to address the harassment of Jewish students and faculty in the months following the Oct. 7, 2023, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  massacre.\par \par \uc2 According to the Justice complaint, the UCLA administration violated Title VII of the 1964 Civil Rights Act when it "turned a blind eye to -- and at times facilitated -- grossly antisemitic acts and systematically ignored cries for help from its own terrified Jewish and Israeli employees." Protesters were allowed to block Jewish students, faculty and staff from parts of the campus, and the school failed to take action to address the antisemitism that threatened professors and others.\par \par \uc2 The school "engaged in a pattern or practice of discrimination against Jewish and Israeli employees at UCLA . . . by failing to prevent and correct antisemitic workplace discrimination," the complaint says. For all the incidents of anti-Jewish harassment, "not a single student, staff member, or faculty member was . . . formally disciplined for antisemitic behavior -- including those who were arrested for illegal conduct."\par \par \uc2 Antisemitism was also apparent in campus hiring, Justice says. "Lots of zionists are applying -- please do your research when you look at applicants and I will also share a doc of no hire list during retreat," Commissioner Alicia Verdugo of UCLA's Undergraduate Students Association Council Cultural Affairs Commission wrote in 2024.\par \par \uc2 UCLA said in a statement that the school had "taken concrete and significant steps to strengthen campus safety, enforce policies and combat antisemitism." But that's hard to credit if UCLA didn't discipline or expel students for behavior that crossed into threats and harassment. Justice says the government "will now do what UCLA has thus far failed to do: protect Jewish and Israeli employees."\par \par \uc2 The suit asks the school to stop tolerating a hostile work environment based on race, religion and national origin. The enforcement is likely to be more severe than if UCLA's leaders had done what is their legal obligation to protect its campus Jewish minority.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: UCLA Treats Antisemitism as the Threat It Is" -- WSJ Mar. 4, 2026)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260227em2r00014\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
