\par\par \b \uc2 Walmart\uc2  to Pay $100 Million In \uc2 FTC\uc2  Case Over Driver Pay\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Morphet \par \uc2 341 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has agreed to pay $100 million to settle \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  claims that the retailer misled delivery drivers about the pay and tips they could earn.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  alleged \uc2 Walmart\uc2  caused drivers with its delivery service, Spark, to lose tens of millions of dollars worth of earnings by showing them inflated figures for what they could expect to earn from pre-tips selected by customers at checkout, base pay and special incentive earnings opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  deceived customers by falsely claiming 100% of customer tips would go to drivers, but the company charged customers for tips that drivers didn't receive, the \uc2 FTC\uc2  said in a complaint filed in federal court in California. Eleven states including North Carolina, Oklahoma and California joined the \uc2 FTC\uc2  on the complaint.\par \par \uc2 "Labor markets cannot function efficiently without truthful and non-misleading information about earnings and other material terms," said Christopher Mufarrige, the FTC's Bureau of Consumer Protection director.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  said it was paying drivers affected by the allegedly deceptive conduct. The company said it valued its drivers and was improving transparency.\par \par \uc2 The big-box retailer allegedly told drivers they would receive the full tip selected by customers at checkout, but tips were split when multiple drivers handled a delivery. The company also lowered the base pay of drivers without warning when orders were removed from drivers making multiple deliveries, the \uc2 FTC\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  alleged drivers weren't paid referral incentives because \uc2 Walmart\uc2  hid the conditions required to earn them, including making the bonuses contingent on new recruits working in specific areas.\par \par \uc2 Regulators have been cracking down on companies they allege have been misleading gig workers. \uc2 Uber Eats\uc2  agreed last month to pay $3.5 million in worker-pay restitution and civil penalties as part of an order by the New York City government, after it was found the food-delivery company failed to pay workers the minimum pay rate between December 2023 and September 2024 for time spent on canceled trips.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260227em2r00026\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
